window.__examData={"exam":"neet","name":"NEET UG","total":366,"sections":[{"name":"Chemistry","count":107,"questions":[{"id":1392,"text":"Which quantum number determines the shape of orbital?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"n"},{"label":"B","text":"l"},{"label":"C","text":"m"},{"label":"D","text":"s"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Azimuthal quantum number (l) determines orbital shape (s=0, p=1, d=2)."},{"id":1393,"text":"The most stable allotrope of carbon is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diamond"},{"label":"B","text":"Graphite"},{"label":"C","text":"Fullerene"},{"label":"D","text":"Coal"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Graphite is the most stable allotrope of carbon at room temperature."},{"id":1394,"text":"Which of these is an example of an aromatic compound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclohexane"},{"label":"B","text":"Benzene"},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclohexene"},{"label":"D","text":"1,3-cyclohexadiene"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Benzene (C6H6) is aromatic with delocalized 6p electrons."},{"id":1395,"text":"The process of electrolysis is based on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chemical effect of current"},{"label":"B","text":"Magnetic effect of current"},{"label":"C","text":"Redox reactions"},{"label":"D","text":"Heating effect"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Electrolysis involves redox reactions at electrodes driven by electric current."},{"id":1396,"text":"Which of the following is a saturated hydrocarbon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C2H4"},{"label":"B","text":"C3H8"},{"label":"C","text":"C2H2"},{"label":"D","text":"C6H6"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Propane (C3H8) follows CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ → saturated alkane."},{"id":1397,"text":"The oxide that turns red litmus blue is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CO2"},{"label":"B","text":"SO2"},{"label":"C","text":"Na2O"},{"label":"D","text":"P2O5"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Na2O is basic oxide → turns red litmus blue. CO2, SO2, P2O5 are acidic."},{"id":1399,"text":"Which of these is a greenhouse gas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N2"},{"label":"B","text":"O2"},{"label":"C","text":"CH4"},{"label":"D","text":"H2"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Methane (CH4) is a potent greenhouse gas (~25× more effective than CO2)."},{"id":1400,"text":"Which of the following has covalent bonding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NaCl"},{"label":"B","text":"H2O"},{"label":"C","text":"KBr"},{"label":"D","text":"CaO"}],"answer":1,"solution":"H2O has covalent bonds (sharing of electrons). NaCl, KBr, CaO are ionic."},{"id":1401,"text":"pH scale ranges from:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0 to 7"},{"label":"B","text":"7 to 14"},{"label":"C","text":"0 to 14"},{"label":"D","text":"1 to 10"}],"answer":2,"solution":"pH scale ranges 0-14 (0-6 acidic, 7 neutral, 8-14 basic)."},{"id":1422,"text":"The element with electronic configuration 1s²2s²2p63s²3p6 belongs to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Group 2"},{"label":"B","text":"Group 18"},{"label":"C","text":"Group 16"},{"label":"D","text":"Group 17"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Full 3p6 → noble gas configuration → Group 18 (Argon)."},{"id":1423,"text":"Which of the following shows paramagnetism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H2"},{"label":"B","text":"N2"},{"label":"C","text":"O2"},{"label":"D","text":"F2"}],"answer":2,"solution":"O2 has two unpaired electrons in its MO configuration → paramagnetic."},{"id":1424,"text":"The IUPAC name of CH3COOH is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Formic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"Ethanoic acid"},{"label":"C","text":"Acetic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"Methanoic acid"}],"answer":1,"solution":"CH3COOH → 2 carbons → Ethanoic acid (common: acetic acid)."},{"id":1425,"text":"The shape of NH3 molecule is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trigonal planar"},{"label":"B","text":"Trigonal pyramidal"},{"label":"C","text":"Tetrahedral"},{"label":"D","text":"Linear"}],"answer":1,"solution":"NH3: 3 bond pairs + 1 lone pair → trigonal pyramidal shape."},{"id":1426,"text":"Which of the following is NOT a polymer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nylon"},{"label":"B","text":"Cellulose"},{"label":"C","text":"Glucose"},{"label":"D","text":"PVC"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Glucose is a monomer (monosaccharide), not a polymer. Nylon, cellulose, PVC are polymers."},{"id":1427,"text":"In a redox reaction, oxidation involves:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of electrons"},{"label":"B","text":"Gain of electrons"},{"label":"C","text":"Gain of hydrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of oxygen"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Oxidation = loss of electrons, Reduction = gain of electrons (OIL RIG)."},{"id":1428,"text":"The bond angle in water is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90°"},{"label":"B","text":"109.5°"},{"label":"C","text":"104.5°"},{"label":"D","text":"120°"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Water has bent shape with 104.5° angle due to lone pair repulsion."},{"id":1429,"text":"Which of these is a double displacement reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2H2 + O2 → 2H2O"},{"label":"B","text":"NaCl + AgNO3 → AgCl + NaNO3"},{"label":"C","text":"Zn + H2SO4 → ZnSO4 + H2"},{"label":"D","text":"CaCO3 → CaO + CO2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Double displacement: two compounds exchange ions → new compounds."},{"id":1430,"text":"The number of moles in 11 g of CO2 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5"},{"label":"B","text":"0.25"},{"label":"C","text":"1"},{"label":"D","text":"2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Molar mass CO2 = 44 g/mol. n = 11/44 = 0.25."},{"id":1431,"text":"The catalyst used in the contact process for H2SO4 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"V2O5"},{"label":"B","text":"Fe"},{"label":"C","text":"Ni"},{"label":"D","text":"Pt"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Contact process uses V2O5 (vanadium pentoxide) as catalyst."},{"id":1447,"text":"The most electronegative element is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"Fluorine"},{"label":"C","text":"Chlorine"},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrogen"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Fluorine (4.0) is the most electronegative element on the Pauling scale."},{"id":1448,"text":"Which gas is produced when zinc reacts with dilute H2SO4?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorine"},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"Carbon dioxide"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Zn + H2SO4 → ZnSO4 + H2↑. Hydrogen gas is produced."},{"id":1449,"text":"Which of the following is a noble gas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydrogen"},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"Argon"},{"label":"D","text":"Chlorine"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Argon (Ar) is a noble gas in Group 18 with a full valence shell."},{"id":1450,"text":"The number of oxygen atoms in 1 mole of H2SO4 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 × 6.022 × 10²³"},{"label":"B","text":"6.022 × 10²³"},{"label":"C","text":"2 × 6.022 × 10²³"},{"label":"D","text":"7 × 6.022 × 10²³"}],"answer":0,"solution":"1 molecule of H2SO4 has 4 O atoms. 1 mole has 4 × NA = 4 × 6.022 × 10²³ atoms."},{"id":1451,"text":"Which bond is present in NaCl?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Covalent bond"},{"label":"B","text":"Metallic bond"},{"label":"C","text":"Ionic bond"},{"label":"D","text":"Hydrogen bond"}],"answer":2,"solution":"NaCl forms an ionic bond between Na⁺ and Cl⁻ through electron transfer."},{"id":1462,"text":"The pH of a neutral solution at 25°C is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"7"},{"label":"C","text":"14"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":1,"solution":"[H⁺] = [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁷ M ⇒ pH = 7 at 25°C."},{"id":1463,"text":"The chemical formula of rust is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"FeO"},{"label":"B","text":"Fe2O3"},{"label":"C","text":"Fe2O3·xH2O"},{"label":"D","text":"Fe3O4"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Rust is hydrated iron(III) oxide, Fe2O3·xH2O."},{"id":1464,"text":"Which catalyst is used in the Haber process for ammonia synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vanadium pentoxide"},{"label":"B","text":"Iron"},{"label":"C","text":"Nickel"},{"label":"D","text":"Platinum"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Haber process uses finely divided iron with promoters (K2O, Al2O3) as catalyst."},{"id":1465,"text":"Which of the following is an isotope of hydrogen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Protium"},{"label":"B","text":"Deuterium"},{"label":"C","text":"Tritium"},{"label":"D","text":"All of these"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Protium (¹H), Deuterium (²H), and Tritium (³H) are all isotopes of hydrogen."},{"id":1466,"text":"The number of electrons in Na? ion is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"11"},{"label":"B","text":"10"},{"label":"C","text":"12"},{"label":"D","text":"9"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Na (11 e⁻) → Na⁺ loses 1 e⁻ → 10 electrons."},{"id":1479,"text":"Which of these is a double displacement reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O"},{"label":"B","text":"NaCl + AgNO₃ → AgCl + NaNO₃"},{"label":"C","text":"Zn + H₂SO₄ → ZnSO₄ + H₂"},{"label":"D","text":"CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Double displacement: two compounds exchange ions → new compounds."},{"id":1485,"text":"Which of the following is a saturated hydrocarbon?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C₂H₄"},{"label":"B","text":"C₃H₈"},{"label":"C","text":"C₂H₂"},{"label":"D","text":"C₆H₆"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Propane (C₃H₈) follows CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ → saturated alkane."},{"id":1486,"text":"Which of the following is NOT a polymer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nylon"},{"label":"B","text":"Cellulose"},{"label":"C","text":"Glucose"},{"label":"D","text":"PVC"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Glucose is a monomer (monosaccharide), not a polymer. Nylon, cellulose, PVC are polymers."},{"id":1487,"text":"Which of the following reagents would distinguish between ethanal and propanone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tollen'sre agent"},{"label":"B","text":"Fehling's solution"},{"label":"C","text":"Benedict's solution"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Ethanal (CH3CHO) is an aldehyde, while propanone (CH3COCH3) is a ketone. Aldehydes can be easily oxidized to carboxylic acids by mild oxidizing agents, whereas ketones are generally resistant to oxidation by mild agents. Tollen'sre agent (ammoniacal silver nitrate), Fehling's solution (alkaline solution of CuSO4 and sodium potassium tartrate), and Benedict's solution (alkaline solution of CuSO4 and sodium citrate) are all mild oxidizing agents used to detect aldehydes.Ethanal, being an aldehyde, will give a positive test with Tollen'sre agent (forming a silver mirror), Fehling's solution (forming a red precipitate of Cu2O), and Benedict's solution (forming a red precipitate of Cu2O).Propanone, being a ketone, will not react with any of these mild oxidizing agents.Therefore, all the listed reagents can distinguish between ethanal and propanone."},{"id":1488,"text":"Which catalyst is used in the Haber process for ammonia synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vanadium pentoxide"},{"label":"B","text":"Iron"},{"label":"C","text":"Nickel"},{"label":"D","text":"Platinum"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Haber process uses finely divided iron with promoters (K₂O, Al₂O₃) as catalyst."},{"id":1491,"text":"Which of the following is a noble gas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydrogen"},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"Argon"},{"label":"D","text":"Chlorine"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Argon (Ar) is a noble gas in Group 18 with a full valence shell."},{"id":1493,"text":"Which of these is a greenhouse gas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N₂"},{"label":"B","text":"O₂"},{"label":"C","text":"CH₄"},{"label":"D","text":"H₂"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Methane (CH₄) is a potent greenhouse gas (~25× more effective than CO₂)."},{"id":1496,"text":"The most stable allotrope of carbon is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diamond"},{"label":"B","text":"Graphite"},{"label":"C","text":"Fullerene"},{"label":"D","text":"Coal"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Graphite is the most stable allotrope of carbon at room temperature."},{"id":1498,"text":"Which of the following shows paramagnetism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H₂"},{"label":"B","text":"N₂"},{"label":"C","text":"O₂"},{"label":"D","text":"F₂"}],"answer":2,"solution":"O₂ has two unpaired electrons in its MO configuration → paramagnetic."},{"id":1508,"text":"The IUPAC name of CHCOOH is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Formic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"Ethanoic acid"},{"label":"C","text":"Acetic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"Methanoic acid"}],"answer":1,"solution":"CHCOOH -> 2 carbons -> Ethanoic acid (common: acetic acid)."},{"id":1511,"text":"The element with electronic configuration 1s^22s^22p3s^23p belongs to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Group 2"},{"label":"B","text":"Group 18"},{"label":"C","text":"Group 16"},{"label":"D","text":"Group 17"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Full 3p -> noble gas configuration -> Group 18 (Argon)."},{"id":1516,"text":"The number of electrons in Nai on is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"11"},{"label":"B","text":"10"},{"label":"C","text":"12"},{"label":"D","text":"9"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Na (11 e) -> Na loses 1 e -> 10 electrons."},{"id":1519,"text":"For the reaction N2(g) + 3H2(g) = 2NH3(g), the partial pressures at a given instant are P(N2) = 0.2 atm, P(H2) = 0.1 atm and P(NH3) = 0.02 atm. What is the reaction quotient Qp at that instant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.25 atm^-2"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 atm^-2"},{"label":"C","text":"10.0 atm^-2"},{"label":"D","text":"0.05 atm^-2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Qp = P(NH3)^2 / [P(N2) x P(H2)^3] = (0.02)^2 / (0.2 x 0.1^3) = 0.0004 / 0.0002 = 2.0 atm^-2. Option B is correct."},{"id":1520,"text":"The pH of a neutral solution at 25 degrees C is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"7"},{"label":"C","text":"14"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":1,"solution":"[H] = [OH] = 10 M -> pH = 7 at 25 degrees C."},{"id":1521,"text":"In a redox reaction, oxidation involves:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Loss of electrons"},{"label":"B","text":"Gain of electrons"},{"label":"C","text":"Gain of hydrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"Loss of oxygen"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Oxidation = loss of electrons, Reduction = gain of electrons (OIL RIG)."},{"id":1522,"text":"The IUPAC name of CH3COCH2COCH3 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pentane-2,4-dione"},{"label":"B","text":"2,4-Pentanedione"},{"label":"C","text":"Pentane-3-one"},{"label":"D","text":"2,4-Dioxopentane"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The given compound is adi ketone. The longest carbon chain containing the two carbonyl groups has five carbons, so the parent alkane is pentane. The carbonyl groups are at positions 2 and 4. Therefore, the IUPAC name is Pentane-2,4-dione. The numbering starts from the end that gives the lower locants to the functional groups."},{"id":1526,"text":"Which gas is produced when zinc reacts with dilute HSO?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorine"},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"Carbon dioxide"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Zn + HSO -> ZnSO + H->. Hydrogen gas is produced."},{"id":1534,"text":"What is the type of reaction that involves the loss of electrons by a species?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxidation reaction"},{"label":"B","text":"Reduction reaction"},{"label":"C","text":"Acid-base reaction"},{"label":"D","text":"Neutralization reaction"}],"answer":0,"solution":"An oxidation reaction is a type of reaction that involves the loss of electrons by a species."},{"id":1540,"text":"Which quantum number determines the shape of orbital?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"n"},{"label":"B","text":"l"},{"label":"C","text":"m"},{"label":"D","text":"s"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Azimuthal quantum number (l) determines orbital shape (s->0, p->1, d->2)."},{"id":1542,"text":"What is the type of bond that occurs between two atoms with a large difference in electronegativity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Covalent bond"},{"label":"B","text":"Ionic bond"},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrogen bond"},{"label":"D","text":"Van der Waals bond"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Ionic bonds occur between two atoms with a large difference in electronegativity, resulting in the transfer of electrons and the formation of ions."},{"id":1547,"text":"Which of these is an example of an aromatic compound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclohexane"},{"label":"B","text":"Benzene"},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclohexene"},{"label":"D","text":"1,3-cyclohexadiene"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Benzene (CH) is aromatic with delocalized 6pi electrons."},{"id":1548,"text":"The oxide that turns red litmus blue is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CO"},{"label":"B","text":"SO"},{"label":"C","text":"NaO"},{"label":"D","text":"PO"}],"answer":2,"solution":"NaO is basic oxide -> turns red litmus blue. CO, SO, PO are acidic."},{"id":1552,"text":"Which of the following is an isotope of hydrogen?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Protium"},{"label":"B","text":"Deuterium"},{"label":"C","text":"Tritium"},{"label":"D","text":"All of these"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Protium (H), Deuterium (^2H), and Tritium (H) are all isotopes of hydrogen."},{"id":1555,"text":"The catalyst used in the contact process for HSO is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"VO"},{"label":"B","text":"Fe"},{"label":"C","text":"Ni"},{"label":"D","text":"Pt"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Contact process uses VO (vanadium pentoxide) as catalyst."},{"id":1560,"text":"What is the type of bond that occurs between two atoms with a large difference in electronegativity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Covalent bond"},{"label":"B","text":"Ionic bond"},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrogen bond"},{"label":"D","text":"Van der Waals bond"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Ionic bonds occur between two atoms with a large difference in electronegativity, resulting in the transfer of electrons and the formation of ions."},{"id":1563,"text":"For the reaction N2(g) + 3H2(g) = 2NH3(g), the partial pressures at a given instant are P(N2) = 0.2 atm, P(H2) = 0.1 atm and P(NH3) = 0.02 atm. What is the reaction quotient Qp at that instant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.25 atm^-2"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0 atm^-2"},{"label":"C","text":"10.0 atm^-2"},{"label":"D","text":"0.05 atm^-2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Qp = P(NH3)^2 / [P(N2) x P(H2)^3] = (0.02)^2 / (0.2 x 0.1^3) = 0.0004 / 0.0002 = 2.0 atm^-2. Option B is correct."},{"id":1574,"text":"pH scale ranges from:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0 to 7"},{"label":"B","text":"7 to 14"},{"label":"C","text":"0 to 14"},{"label":"D","text":"1 to 10"}],"answer":2,"solution":"pH scale ranges 0-14 (0-6 acidic, 7 neutral, 8-14 basic)."},{"id":1577,"text":"What is the type of reaction that involves the loss of electrons by a species?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxidation reaction"},{"label":"B","text":"Reduction reaction"},{"label":"C","text":"Acid-base reaction"},{"label":"D","text":"Neutralization reaction"}],"answer":0,"solution":"An oxidation reaction is a type of reaction that involves the loss of electrons by a species."},{"id":1584,"text":"The IUPAC name of CHCOOH is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Formic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"Ethanoic acid"},{"label":"C","text":"Acetic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"Methanoic acid"}],"answer":1,"solution":"CHCOOH -> 2 carbons -> Ethanoic acid (common: acetic acid)."},{"id":1586,"text":"What is the type of isomerism that occurs when molecules have the same molecular formula and sequence of bonded atoms, but differ in the three-dimensional arrangement of their atoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Structural isomerism"},{"label":"B","text":"Stereoisomerism"},{"label":"C","text":"Enantiomerism"},{"label":"D","text":"Tautomerism"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Stereoisomerism occurs when molecules have the same molecular formula and sequence of bonded atoms, but differ in the three-dimensional arrangement of their atoms."},{"id":1593,"text":"Which gas is produced when zinc reacts with dilute HSO?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxygen"},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorine"},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrogen"},{"label":"D","text":"Carbon dioxide"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Zn + HSO -> ZnSO + H->. Hydrogen gas is produced."},{"id":1596,"text":"Which of the following has covalent bonding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NaCl"},{"label":"B","text":"HO"},{"label":"C","text":"KBr"},{"label":"D","text":"CaO"}],"answer":1,"solution":"HO has covalent bonds (sharing of electrons). NaCl, KBr, CaO are ionic."},{"id":1597,"text":"The most stable allotrope of carbon is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diamond"},{"label":"B","text":"Graphite"},{"label":"C","text":"Fullerene"},{"label":"D","text":"Coal"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Graphite is the most stable allotrope of carbon at room temperature."},{"id":1598,"text":"The bond angle in water is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90 degrees"},{"label":"B","text":"109.5 degrees"},{"label":"C","text":"104.5 degrees"},{"label":"D","text":"120 degrees"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Water has bent shape with 104.5 degrees  angle due to lone pair repulsion."},{"id":1601,"text":"The number of oxygen atoms in 1 mole of HSO is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4 x 6.022 x 10^2"},{"label":"B","text":"6.022 x 10^2"},{"label":"C","text":"2 x 6.022 x 10^2"},{"label":"D","text":"7 x 6.022 x 10^2"}],"answer":0,"solution":"1 molecule of HSO has 4 O atoms. 1 mole has 4 x NA = 4 x 6.022 x 10^2 atoms."},{"id":1604,"text":"Which of the following reagents would distinguish between ethanal and propanone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tollen'sre agent"},{"label":"B","text":"Fehling's solution"},{"label":"C","text":"Benedict's solution"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Ethanal (CH3CHO) is an aldehyde, while propanone (CH3COCH3) is a ketone. Aldehydes can be easily oxidized to carboxylic acids by mild oxidizing agents, whereas ketones are generally resistant to oxidation by mild agents. Tollen'sre agent (ammoniacal silver nitrate), Fehling's solution (alkaline solution of CuSO4 and sodium potassium tartrate), and Benedict's solution (alkaline solution of CuSO4 and sodium citrate) are all mild oxidizing agents used to detect aldehydes.\r\nEthanal, being an aldehyde, will give a positive test with Tollen'sre agent (forming a silver mirror), Fehling's solution (forming a red precipitate of Cu2O), and Benedict's solution (forming a red precipitate of Cu2O).\r\nPropanone, being a ketone, will not react with any of these mild oxidizing agents.\r\nTherefore, all the listed reagents can distinguish between ethanal and propanone."},{"id":1606,"text":"The number of electrons in Nai on is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"11"},{"label":"B","text":"10"},{"label":"C","text":"12"},{"label":"D","text":"9"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Na (11 e) -> Na loses 1 e -> 10 electrons."},{"id":1608,"text":"Which of the following is a noble gas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hydrogen"},{"label":"B","text":"Nitrogen"},{"label":"C","text":"Argon"},{"label":"D","text":"Chlorine"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Argon (Ar) is a noble gas in Group 18 with a full valence shell."},{"id":1610,"text":"Which of the following is NOT a polymer?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nylon"},{"label":"B","text":"Cellulose"},{"label":"C","text":"Glucose"},{"label":"D","text":"PVC"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Glucose is a monomer (monosaccharide), not a polymer. Nylon, cellulose, PVC are polymers."},{"id":1618,"text":"Which of the following reactions represents a Williamson ether synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R-OH + Na -> R-ONa + 1/2 H"},{"label":"B","text":"R-ONa + R'-X -> R-O-R' + NaX"},{"label":"C","text":"R-OH + HX -> R-X + HO"},{"label":"D","text":"R-CHO + R'-MgBr -> R-CH(OMgBr)-R'"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Williamson ether synthesis involves the reaction of an alkoxide (formed from an alcohol and a strong base like Na) with an alkyl halide to form an ether. Option B correctly depicts this reaction."},{"id":1623,"text":"The process of electrolysis is based on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chemical effect of current"},{"label":"B","text":"Magnetic effect of current"},{"label":"C","text":"Redox reactions"},{"label":"D","text":"Heating effect"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Electrolysis involves redox reactions at electrodes driven by electric current."},{"id":1630,"text":"The oxide that turns red litmus blue is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CO"},{"label":"B","text":"SO"},{"label":"C","text":"NaO"},{"label":"D","text":"PO"}],"answer":2,"solution":"NaO is basic oxide -> turns red litmus blue. CO, SO, PO are acidic."},{"id":1631,"text":"The shape of NH molecule is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trigonal planar"},{"label":"B","text":"Trigonal pyramidal"},{"label":"C","text":"Tetrahedral"},{"label":"D","text":"Linear"}],"answer":1,"solution":"NH: 3 bond pairs + 1 lone pair -> trigonal pyramidal shape."},{"id":1632,"text":"What is the hybridization of the carbon atom in methane?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"sp"},{"label":"B","text":"sp2"},{"label":"C","text":"sp3"},{"label":"D","text":"sp3d"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The carbon atom in methane is bonded to four hydrogen atoms, and its electronic configuration is 1s2 2s2 2p2. To form four equivalent bonds, the carbon atom undergoes sp3 hybridization."},{"id":1639,"text":"The bond angle in water is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90 degrees"},{"label":"B","text":"109.5 degrees"},{"label":"C","text":"104.5 degrees"},{"label":"D","text":"120 degrees"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Water has bent shape with 104.5 degrees  angle due to lone pair repulsion."},{"id":1645,"text":"What is the type of reaction that involves the combination of two or more substances to form a new compound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Synthesis reaction"},{"label":"B","text":"Decomposition reaction"},{"label":"C","text":"Replacement reaction"},{"label":"D","text":"Combustion reaction"}],"answer":0,"solution":"A synthesis reaction is a type of chemical reaction that involves the combination of two or more substances to form a new compound. This type of reaction is also known as a direct combination reaction."},{"id":1646,"text":"Which of the following shows paramagnetism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"H"},{"label":"B","text":"N"},{"label":"C","text":"O"},{"label":"D","text":"F"}],"answer":2,"solution":"O has two unpaired electrons in its MO configuration -> paramagnetic."},{"id":1647,"text":"What is the type of reaction that involves the breakdown of a compound into simpler substances using a catalyst?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Synthesis reaction"},{"label":"B","text":"Decomposition reaction"},{"label":"C","text":"Replacement reaction"},{"label":"D","text":"Catalytic reaction"}],"answer":3,"solution":"A catalytic reaction is a type of chemical reaction that involves the breakdown of a compound into simpler substances using a catalyst. The catalyst speeds up the reaction without being consumed or altered in the process."},{"id":1648,"text":"What is the type of bond that holds atoms together in a molecule?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ionic bond"},{"label":"B","text":"Covalent bond"},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrogen bond"},{"label":"D","text":"Van der Waals bond"}],"answer":1,"solution":"A covalent bond is a type of chemical bond that holds atoms together in a molecule. It is formed when two or more atoms share one or more pairs of electrons in order to achieve a more stable electronic configuration."},{"id":1651,"text":"What is the type of bond that occurs between two atoms with a large difference in electronegativity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Covalent bond"},{"label":"B","text":"Ionic bond"},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrogen bond"},{"label":"D","text":"Van der Waals bond"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Ionic bonds occur between two atoms with a large difference in electronegativity, resulting in the transfer of electrons and the formation of ions."},{"id":1676,"text":"For a reaction, ΔH = +50 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔS = +150 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. The minimum temperature (in K) above which the reaction becomes spontaneous is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"333 K"},{"label":"B","text":"33.3 K"},{"label":"C","text":"300 K"},{"label":"D","text":"500 K"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Spontaneity requires ΔG < 0. At equilibrium ΔG = 0 = ΔH - TΔS → T = ΔH/ΔS. Convert ΔH to J: 50,000 J mol⁻¹. T = 50,000 / 150 = 333.33 K. Reaction is spontaneous for T > 333 K. Full Solution: ΔG = ΔH - TΔS. For spontaneity, ΔG < 0 ⇒ TΔS > ΔH ⇒ T > ΔH/ΔS. ΔH = 50 kJ mol⁻¹ = 50,000 J mol⁻¹. ΔS = 150 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. T = 50,000 J mol⁻¹ / 150 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ = 1000/3 K ≈ 333.33 K."},{"id":1677,"text":"An element with molar mass 27 g mol⁻¹ forms a cubic unit cell with edge length 4.05 × 10⁻⁸ cm. If its density is 2.7 g cm⁻³, the number of atoms per unit cell (Z) is approximately: (Use N_A = 6 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Use ρ = ZM / (N_A a³) → Z = ρ N_A a³ / M. Approximate a³ ≈ (4 × 10⁻⁸)³ = 64 × 10⁻²⁴ cm³. Z ≈ (2.7 × 6 × 10²³ × 64 × 10⁻²⁴) / 27 = (2.7 × 6 × 64 × 10⁻¹) / 27 = (103.68) / 27 ≈ 3.84 ≈ 4 (FCC). Full Solution: a = 4.05 × 10⁻⁸ cm. a³ = 66.43 × 10⁻²⁴ cm³. Z = (2.7 g cm⁻³ × 6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹ × 66.43 × 10⁻²⁴ cm³) / 27 g mol⁻¹ = (2.7 × 6.022 × 66.43 × 10⁻¹) / 27 ≈ 107.9 / 27 ≈ 3.996 ≈ 4."},{"id":1678,"text":"The molar conductivity of a 0.01 M solution of a weak electrolyte is 20 S cm² mol⁻¹. If its limiting molar conductivity (Λ°_m) is 400 S cm² mol⁻¹, the degree of dissociation (α) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.005"},{"label":"B","text":"0.05"},{"label":"C","text":"0.5"},{"label":"D","text":"0.02"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For weak electrolytes, degree of dissociation α = Λ_m / Λ°_m (Ostwald's dilution law approximation). α = 20 / 400 = 0.05. Full Solution: The relationship between molar conductivity at concentration 'c' (Λ_m) and limiting molar conductivity (Λ°_m) for a weak electrolyte is given by α = Λ_m / Λ°_m. Substituting values: α = 20 S cm² mol⁻¹ / 400 S cm² mol⁻¹ = 1/20 = 0.05."},{"id":1679,"text":"The half-life of a first-order reaction is 20 minutes. The time required for the concentration of the reactant to drop to 12.5% of its initial value is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20 minutes"},{"label":"B","text":"40 minutes"},{"label":"C","text":"60 minutes"},{"label":"D","text":"80 minutes"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: 12.5% = 1/8 = (1/2)³, meaning 3 half-lives have passed. Time = 3 × 20 min = 60 min. Full Solution: For a first-order reaction, fraction remaining = (1/2)ⁿ where n = number of half-lives. (1/2)ⁿ = 1/8 = (1/2)³ ⇒ n = 3. Total time t = n × t₁/₂ = 3 × 20 min = 60 min."},{"id":1691,"text":"For the reaction N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), Kc is 4.0 × 10⁻² at 500 K. The value of Kp at the same temperature is: (R = 0.0821 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.6 × 10⁻⁴"},{"label":"B","text":"2.4 × 10⁻⁵"},{"label":"C","text":"6.0 × 10⁻³"},{"label":"D","text":"4.0 × 10⁻²"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Δn_g = 2 - (1+3) = -2. Kp = Kc/(RT)². RT ≈ 0.082 × 500 = 41. (41)² ≈ 1681. Kp ≈ 0.04/1681 ≈ 2.38 × 10⁻⁵. Full: Δn_g = moles gaseous products - moles gaseous reactants = 2 - 4 = -2. Kp = Kc(RT)^Δn_g = 4.0×10⁻² × (0.0821×500)⁻² = 4.0×10⁻² / (41.05)² = 4.0×10⁻² / 1685.1 ≈ 2.37×10⁻⁵."},{"id":1692,"text":"The osmotic pressure (π) of a 0.01 M aqueous solution of K₃[Fe(CN)₆] at 300 K is measured. Assuming complete dissociation, the value of π/RT is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.01 mol L⁻¹"},{"label":"B","text":"0.02 mol L⁻¹"},{"label":"C","text":"0.03 mol L⁻¹"},{"label":"D","text":"0.04 mol L⁻¹"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: K₃[Fe(CN)₆] → 3K⁺ + [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ gives 4 ions, so van't Hoff factor i = 4. π/RT = i × C = 4 × 0.01 = 0.04. Full: Osmotic pressure π = iCRT. For complete dissociation of K₃[Fe(CN)₆], total ions per formula unit = 4, hence i = 4. Therefore, π/RT = iC = 4 × 0.01 M = 0.04 mol L⁻¹."},{"id":1693,"text":"A transition metal complex exhibits as pin-only magnetic moment of 3.87 BM. The number of unpaired electrons in the complex is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Memorize spin-only values: n=1 (1.73), n=2 (2.83), n=3 (3.87), n=4 (4.90), n=5 (5.92). 3.87 BM corresponds directly to n=3. Full: Spin-only formula μ = √[n(n+2)] BM. Given μ = 3.87, squaring gives n² + 2n = 15 → n² + 2n - 15 = 0 → (n+5)(n-3) = 0 → n = 3 (rejecting negative value)."},{"id":1694,"text":"What is the pH of a 0.01 M aqueous solution of acetic acid (Ka = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵) at 298 K?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.87"},{"label":"B","text":"3.37"},{"label":"C","text":"3.87"},{"label":"D","text":"4.37"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For a weak acid HA with Ka << C, [H⁺] ≈ √(Ka·C); pH = ½(pKa – log C). Here pKa = 4.74, log C = –2 → pH = ½(4.74 + 2) = 3.37. Full method: Write Ka = x²/(C–x) ≈ x²/C, solve x = √(1.8×10⁻⁵ × 0.01) = 4.24×10⁻⁴ M, pH = –log(4.24×10⁻⁴) = 3.37."},{"id":1706,"text":"The unit of the rate constant for a reaction is L² mol⁻² s⁻¹. The overall order of the reaction is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zero"},{"label":"B","text":"First"},{"label":"C","text":"Second"},{"label":"D","text":"Third"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Unit of rate constant k = (concentration)^(1-n) (time)^-1. Given unit is L² mol⁻² s⁻¹ = M⁻² s⁻¹. Equate exponent of concentration: 1 - n = -2 → n = 3. Full Solution: For an nth order reaction, Rate = k[A]^n. Units: M s⁻¹ = k × M^n → k = M^(1-n) s⁻¹. Given k = M⁻² s⁻¹. Therefore, 1 - n = -2 → n = 3."},{"id":1707,"text":"The total number of stereoisomers (geometrical + optical) possible for the octahedral complex [Co(en)2Cl2]⁺ (where en = ethylenediamine) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For [M(AA)2B2] type octahedral complexes (AA = bidentate ligand), there are 3 stereoisomers: one trans (optically inactive) and a pair ofc is-enantiomers (optically active). Full Solution: The complex exhibits geometrical isomerism (cis and trans). The trans isomer has a plane of symmetry, hence optically inactive (1 isomer). The cis isomer lacks symmetry elements (plane/center), exists as an on-superimposable mirror image pair (d and l forms), giving 2 optical isomers. Total = 1 (trans) + 2 (cis pair) = 3 stereoisomers."},{"id":1708,"text":"The pH of a 1.0 × 10⁻⁸ M aqueous HCl solution at 298 K is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8.00"},{"label":"B","text":"7.00"},{"label":"C","text":"6.98"},{"label":"D","text":"8.02"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: For very dilute strong acid (C < 10⁻⁶ M), [H⁺]total = C_acid + 10⁻⁷ (from water autoionization). [H⁺] = 10⁻⁸ + 10⁻⁷ = 1.1 × 10⁻⁷ M. pH = -αlog(1.1 × 10⁻⁷) = 7 - αlog(1.1) ≈ 7 - 0.04 = 6.96 ≈ 6.98. Full Solution: HCl is a strong acid, [H⁺]_HCl = 10⁻⁸ M. Water contributes [H⁺]_w = 10⁻⁷ M. Total [H⁺] = 10⁻⁸ + 10⁻⁷ = 1.1 × 10⁻⁷ M. pH = -αlog(1.1 × 10⁻⁷) = 6.9586 ≈ 6.98. pH cannot be >7 for an acidic solution."},{"id":1709,"text":"A buffer solution is prepared by mixing 0.1 M acetic acid (CH₃COOH) and 0.2 M sodium acetate (CH₃COONa). Given that the pKa of acetic acid is 4.74, what is the pH of this buffer solution?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4.44"},{"label":"B","text":"4.74"},{"label":"C","text":"5.04"},{"label":"D","text":"5.34"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Use Henderson-Hasselbalch equation pH = pKa + log([Salt]/[Acid]). Ratio [Salt]/[Acid] = 0.2/0.1 = 2. log₁₀(2) ≈ 0.3. pH = 4.74 + 0.3 = 5.04. Full Solution: pKa = 4.74. [CH₃COONa] = 0.2 M, [CH₃COOH] = 0.1 M. pH = 4.74 + log(0.2/0.1) = 4.74 + log 2 = 4.74 + 0.3010 = 5.041 ≈ 5.04."},{"id":1721,"text":"The complex ion [Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ is dissolved in water. What is the oxidation state of cobalt in this complex?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+1"},{"label":"B","text":"+2"},{"label":"C","text":"+3"},{"label":"D","text":"+4"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Recognize that NH₃ is a neutral ligand and Cl⁻ is the counter‑ion. Total charge of complex = +3 (from three Cl⁻). Hence oxidation state of Co = +3. Full: Let x be oxidation state of Co. Sum of charges: x + 6(0) + 3(–1) = 0 (overall neutral salt). So x – 3 = 0 → x = +3. Therefore option C is correct."},{"id":1722,"text":"The rate constant of a reaction becomes 4 times when the temperature is increased from 300 K to 320 K. The activation energy (Ea) of the reaction in kJ mol⁻¹ is approximately: (Use R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, log 2 = 0.301)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"55.2"},{"label":"B","text":"42.5"},{"label":"C","text":"68.3"},{"label":"D","text":"31.8"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Use log₁₀ Arrhenius form: log(k₂/k₁) = (Eₐ/2.303R)(1/T₁ - 1/T₂). Here k₂/k₁=4, log4=0.602. Δ(1/T) = (320-300)/(300×320) = 1/4800. Eₐ = (0.602 × 2.303 × 8.314 × 4800) / 1000 ≈ 55.2 kJ mol⁻¹. Full: ln(k₂/k₁) = (Eₐ/R)(1/T₁ - 1/T₂). ln4 = 1.386. Eₐ = (1.386 × 8.314 × 4800) / 1000 = 55.2 kJ mol⁻¹."},{"id":1723,"text":"The solubility product (Ksp) of Ag₂CrO₄ is 1.1 × 10⁻¹² at 298 K. The molar solubility of Ag₂CrO₄ in 0.1 M AgNO₃ solution (in mol L⁻¹) is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.1 × 10⁻¹⁰"},{"label":"B","text":"1.1 × 10⁻¹²"},{"label":"C","text":"6.5 × 10⁻⁵"},{"label":"D","text":"1.1 × 10⁻⁶"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Common ion effect. [Ag⁺] ≈ 0.1 M from AgNO₃. Ksp = [Ag⁺]²[CrO₄²⁻] = (0.1)² × s. s = Ksp / 0.01 = 1.1×10⁻¹² / 10⁻² = 1.1×10⁻¹⁰ M. Option elimination: Pure water solubility (Option C) is ~10⁻⁵ M; common ion suppresses it by ~10⁵ times. Full: Let s = solubility. [Ag⁺] = 0.1 + 2s ≈ 0.1. [CrO₄²⁻] = s. Ksp = (0.1)²(s) = 1.1×10⁻¹² → s = 1.1×10⁻¹⁰ M."},{"id":1724,"text":"For the cell reaction: Cu(s) + 2Ag⁺(aq) → Cu²⁺(aq) + 2Ag(s), the standard cell potential E°cell is 0.46 V at 298 K. The equilibrium constant (Kc) for the reaction is of the order of: (Use 2.303RT/F = 0.059 V)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10¹⁰"},{"label":"B","text":"10¹⁵"},{"label":"C","text":"10²⁰"},{"label":"D","text":"10⁵"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: At equilibrium, E°cell = (0.059/n) log Kc. n=2. log Kc = (n × E°cell) / 0.059 = (2 × 0.46) / 0.059 = 0.92 / 0.059 ≈ 15.6. Kc ≈ 10¹⁵.⁶, order 10¹⁵. Full: ΔG° = -nFE° = -RT ln Kc. ln Kc = nFE°/RT. log Kc = nE°/(0.059) = 15.59. Kc = 3.9 × 10¹⁵."},{"id":1736,"text":"An element crystallizes in a face-centered cubic (FCC) lattice with a cell edge length of 400 pm. If the density of the element is 8.0 g cm⁻³, the atomic mass of the element is approximately: (N_A = 6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"52 g mol⁻¹"},{"label":"B","text":"77 g mol⁻¹"},{"label":"C","text":"104 g mol⁻¹"},{"label":"D","text":"154 g mol⁻¹"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For FCC, Z=4. Use ρ = ZM/Nₐa³ → M = ρNₐa³/Z. Approx Nₐ≈6×10²³, a=4×10⁻⁸ cm. M ≈ (8×6×10²³×64×10⁻²⁴)/4 = 76.8 ≈ 77 g/mol. Full Solution: M = (8 g/cm³ × 6.022×10²³ mol⁻¹ × (4×10⁻⁸ cm)³) / 4 = (8 × 6.022 × 64 × 10⁻¹) / 4 = 77.1 g/mol."},{"id":1737,"text":"The molar conductivities at infinite dilution (Λ°ₘ) for NaCl, HCl, and CH₃COONa are 126, 426, and 91 S cm² mol⁻¹ respectively. The Λ°ₘ for CH₃COOH (in S cm² mol⁻¹) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"391"},{"label":"B","text":"291"},{"label":"C","text":"491"},{"label":"D","text":"591"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Kohlrausch's Law: Λ°(CH₃COOH) = Λ°(HCl) + Λ°(CH₃COONa) – Λ°(NaCl) = 426 + 91 – 126 = 391 S cm² mol⁻¹. Full Solution: Λ°(CH₃COOH) = λ°(H⁺) + λ°(CH₃COO⁻). From given data: λ°(H⁺)+λ°(Cl⁻)=426; λ°(Na⁺)+λ°(CH₃COO⁻)=91; λ°(Na⁺)+λ°(Cl⁻)=126. Adding first two and subtracting third gives λ°(H⁺)+λ°(CH₃COO⁻) = 391."},{"id":1738,"text":"Which of the following compounds will NOT undergo aldol condensation but WILL undergo Cannizzaro reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetaldehyde"},{"label":"B","text":"Propanone"},{"label":"C","text":"Butanal"},{"label":"D","text":"Benzaldehyde"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Aldol condensation requires α-hydrogen; Cannizzaro reaction occurs for non-enolizable aldehydes (no α-H). Benzaldehyde (C₆H₅CHO) has no α-H. Full Solution: Acetaldehyde (CH₃CHO), Propanone (CH₃COCH₃), and Butanal (CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO) possess α-hydrogens and undergo aldol condensation. Benzaldehyde lacks α-hydrogens, so it cannot form an enolate for aldol but undergoes Cannizzaro reaction (disproportionation) in concentrated alkali."},{"id":1739,"text":"For a first-order reaction, the time required for 75% completion is 60 minutes. The time required for 93.75% completion (in minutes) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"90"},{"label":"B","text":"120"},{"label":"C","text":"150"},{"label":"D","text":"100"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: 75% completion = 2 half-lives (t₁/₂). 93.75% completion = 4 half-lives. Given 2 t₁/₂ = 60 min → t₁/₂ = 30 min → 4 t₁/₂ = 120 min. Full Solution: For 1st order, fraction remaining = (1/2)ⁿ. 75% done → 25% left = (1/2)² → n=2. 93.75% done → 6.25% left = (1/2)⁴ → n=4. Time is proportional to number of half-lives. t₉₃.₇₅% = (4/2) × 60 = 120 min."},{"id":1751,"text":"For a certain reaction at 298 K, the standard Gibbs free energy change (ΔG°) is -11.4 kJ mol⁻¹. The value of the equilibrium constant (K) for the reaction is approximately: (Use R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, ln 10 = 2.303)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10"},{"label":"B","text":"100"},{"label":"C","text":"1000"},{"label":"D","text":"10000"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: At 298 K, 2.303RT ≈ 5.7 kJ mol⁻¹. Use log K = -ΔG°/5.7 = 11.4/5.7 = 2 → K = 10² = 100. Step-by-step: ΔG° = -2.303 RT log K. Substituting ΔG° = -11400 J mol⁻¹, R = 8.314, T = 298 gives 2.303 × 8.314 × 298 ≈ 5705 J mol⁻¹. log K = 11400 / 5705 = 2. Therefore, K = 100."},{"id":1752,"text":"0.5 g of an on-volatile solute (molar mass 100 g mol⁻¹) is dissolved in 50 g of water. The freezing point of the solution is -0.186°C. The Van't Hoff factor (i) for the solute is: (Kf for water = 1.86 K kg mol⁻¹)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Calculate theoretical ΔTf for i=1: ΔTf = Kf × m. Molality m = (0.5/100) / 0.05 = 0.1 m. ΔTf = 1.86 × 0.1 = 0.186 K. Matches observed ΔTf exactly, so i = 1. Step-by-step: Moles of solute = 0.5 g / 100 g mol⁻¹ = 0.005 mol. Mass of solvent = 50 g = 0.05 kg. Molality = 0.005 / 0.05 = 0.1 mol kg⁻¹. Observed ΔTf = 0.186 K. Using ΔTf = i × Kf × m → 0.186 = i × 1.86 × 0.1 → i = 0.186 / 0.186 = 1."},{"id":1753,"text":"The EMF of a concentration cell Cu(s) | Cu²⁺(0.1 M) || Cu²⁺(0.01 M) | Cu(s) at 298 K is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.0591 V"},{"label":"B","text":"0.0295 V"},{"label":"C","text":"0.0118 V"},{"label":"D","text":"0.0 V"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For a concentration cell with identical electrodes, E = (0.0591/n) log₁₀(cathode/anode); here n=2 and concentration ratio = 10, giving E = 0.0591/2 × 1 = 0.02955 V. Step‑by‑step: Apply the Nernst equation E = (0.0591/2) log₁₀(0.1/0.01) = 0.02955 V ≈ 0.0295 V."},{"id":1754,"text":"For a zero‑order reaction, the half‑life is 20 minutes when the initial concentration is 0.2 M. What is the half‑life when the initial concentration is 0.5 M?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8 min"},{"label":"B","text":"20 min"},{"label":"C","text":"50 min"},{"label":"D","text":"100 min"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: In zero‑order kinetics t₁/₂ ∝ [A]₀; thus t₁/₂(new) = 20 min × (0.5/0.2) = 50 min. Step‑by‑step: Using t₁/₂ = [A]₀/(2k), the rate constant k remains unchanged, so t₁/₂,₂ = t₁/₂,₁ × ([A]₀,₂/[A]₀,₁) = 20 × 2.5 = 50 min."},{"id":1766,"text":"Which of the following molecules has a net dipole moment of zero?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NH₃"},{"label":"B","text":"H₂O"},{"label":"C","text":"BF₃"},{"label":"D","text":"SO₂"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: A molecule has zero net dipole moment if its geometry is symmetrical and all terminal atoms are identical. BF₃ is trigonal planar (sp² hybridised, no lone pairs on B) with three identical B–F bonds; the bond dipoles cancel vectorially. NH₃ (pyramidal), H₂O (bent), and SO₂ (bent) have lone pairs causing asymmetry and net dipole moments. Step-by-step: Determine hybridisation and shape using VSEPR. BF₃: Steric number 3, trigonal planar, symmetrical → μ = 0. NH₃: Steric number 4 (1 lp), pyramidal → μ > 0. H₂O: Steric number 4 (2 lp), bent → μ > 0. SO₂: Steric number 3 (1 lp), bent → μ > 0."},{"id":1767,"text":"Using the given average bond enthalpies (in kJ mol⁻¹): C–H = 413, C–C = 348, C=C = 614, H–H = 436, the enthalpy change (ΔH) for the reaction CH₂=CH₂(g) + H₂(g) → CH₃–CH₃(g) is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-124 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"label":"B","text":"+124 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"label":"C","text":"-248 kJ mol⁻¹"},{"label":"D","text":"+248 kJ mol⁻¹"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: ΔH ≈ Σ(Bond enthalpies of bonds broken) – Σ(Bond enthalpies of bonds formed). Bonds broken: 1 C=C (614) + 1 H–H (436) = 1050 kJ mol⁻¹. Bonds formed: 1 C–C (348) + 2 C–H (2×413 = 826) = 1174 kJ mol⁻¹. ΔH = 1050 – 1174 = –124 kJ mol⁻¹. Step-by-step: Draw structures. Reactants: one C=C, one H–H, four C–H (unchanged). Products: one C–C, six C–H. Net change: Break C=Cand H–H; Form C–Cand two C–H. Calculation yields –124 kJ mol⁻¹."},{"id":1768,"text":"The pKa of a weak acid HA is 4.0 and the pKb of a weak base BOH is 5.0. The pH of a 0.1 M solution of the salt BA (formed from HA and BOH) at 298 K is: (Given Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7.0"},{"label":"B","text":"6.5"},{"label":"C","text":"7.5"},{"label":"D","text":"5.5"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For a salt of weak acid and weak base, pH = 7 + ½(pKa – pKb). This is independent of concentration. pH = 7 + ½(4.0 – 5.0) = 7 – 0.5 = 6.5. Step-by-step: Hydrolysis constants Kh = Kw/KaKb. [H⁺]"},{"id":1769,"text":"The spin-only magnetic moment (in Bohr magneton) of the complex ion [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ is approximately: (Atomic number of Fe = 26)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.73 BM"},{"label":"B","text":"2.83 BM"},{"label":"C","text":"3.87 BM"},{"label":"D","text":"5.92 BM"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Fe is in +3 oxidation state (d⁵). CN⁻ is a strong field ligand causing low-spin pairing (t₂g⁵ eg⁰), leaving 1 unpaired electron. Apply μ = √[n(n+2)] = √3 ≈ 1.73 BM. Step-by-step: Oxidation state of Fe = +3 (since 6×(-1) + x = -3). Electronic configuration of Fe³⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁵. CN⁻ is a strong field ligand (spectrochemical series), so pairing occurs in t₂g orbitals (t₂g⁵ eg⁰). Number of unpaired electrons (n) = 1. Spin-only magnetic moment = √[1(1+2)] = √3 = 1.732 BM."}]},{"name":"Physics","count":103,"questions":[{"id":1383,"text":"A force of 10 N acts on a body of mass 2 kgf or 5 s. The impulse is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 Ns"},{"label":"B","text":"50 Ns"},{"label":"C","text":"2 Ns"},{"label":"D","text":"25 Ns"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Impulse = F × t = 10 × 5 = 50 Ns."},{"id":1384,"text":"The internal resistance of an ideal voltmeter is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zero"},{"label":"B","text":"Low"},{"label":"C","text":"Infinity"},{"label":"D","text":"1 Ω"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Ideal voltmeter has infinite resistance to draw zero current."},{"id":1385,"text":"The phenomenon of splitting of light into its constituent colors is called:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reflection"},{"label":"B","text":"Refraction"},{"label":"C","text":"Dispersion"},{"label":"D","text":"Diffraction"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Dispersion splits white light into VIBGYOR colors (e.g., rainbow)."},{"id":1386,"text":"Which law states that entropy of an isolated system always increases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First law"},{"label":"B","text":"Second law"},{"label":"C","text":"Third law"},{"label":"D","text":"Zeroth law"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Second law: entropy of isolated system always increases (?S = 0)."},{"id":1387,"text":"The time period of a simple pendulum depends on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mass of bob"},{"label":"B","text":"Amplitude (small)"},{"label":"C","text":"Length of pendulum"},{"label":"D","text":"Material of bob"}],"answer":2,"solution":"T = 2pv(L/g). Time period depends only on length and g."},{"id":1388,"text":"The magnetic field at the center of a current carrying circular loop is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zero"},{"label":"B","text":"µ0I/2R"},{"label":"C","text":"µ0I/R"},{"label":"D","text":"µ0I/4pR"}],"answer":1,"solution":"B = µ0I/(2R) at center of circular loop."},{"id":1390,"text":"The direction of induced current is given by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampere's law"},{"label":"B","text":"Biot-Savart law"},{"label":"C","text":"Lenz's law"},{"label":"D","text":"Ohm's law"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Lenz's law determines direction: induced current opposes change in flux."},{"id":1391,"text":"Radioactive decay follows which order of kinetics?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zero order"},{"label":"B","text":"First order"},{"label":"C","text":"Second order"},{"label":"D","text":"Third order"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Radioactive decay follows first order kinetics: N = N0e???."},{"id":1413,"text":"The dimension of Planck's constant is same as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Energy"},{"label":"B","text":"Angular momentum"},{"label":"C","text":"Force"},{"label":"D","text":"Power"}],"answer":1,"solution":"h = E/ν ⇒ [ML²T⁻¹] same as angular momentum."},{"id":1414,"text":"A wire of length Land area A has resistance R. If length is halved and area doubled, new resistance is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R/2"},{"label":"B","text":"R/4"},{"label":"C","text":"R"},{"label":"D","text":"2R"}],"answer":1,"solution":"R' = ρ(L/2)/(2A) = (ρL/A)/4 = R/4."},{"id":1415,"text":"The angle between electric dipole moment and electric field in stable equilibrium is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0°"},{"label":"B","text":"90°"},{"label":"C","text":"180°"},{"label":"D","text":"45°"}],"answer":0,"solution":"In stable equilibrium, p is parallel to E ⇒ θ = 0°, torque = 0."},{"id":1416,"text":"The energy equivalent of 1 amu is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"931 eV"},{"label":"B","text":"931 MeV"},{"label":"C","text":"931 keV"},{"label":"D","text":"931 J"}],"answer":1,"solution":"1 amu = 931.5 MeV/c²."},{"id":1418,"text":"A body of mass 5 kg is moving with momentum 20 kg m/s. Its velocity is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"4 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"100 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"0.25 m/s"}],"answer":1,"solution":"v = p/m = 20/5 = 4 m/s."},{"id":1419,"text":"In Young's double slit experiment, fringe width depends on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Wavelength only"},{"label":"B","text":"Slit width"},{"label":"C","text":"Wavelength, slit distance, and screen distance"},{"label":"D","text":"Screen distance only"}],"answer":2,"solution":"β = λD/d. Depends on λ, D (screen distance), and d (slit separation)."},{"id":1420,"text":"The coefficient of performance of a refrigerator is given by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Q1/W"},{"label":"B","text":"Q2/W"},{"label":"C","text":"W/Q1"},{"label":"D","text":"(Q1-Q2)/W"}],"answer":1,"solution":"COP = Q2/W where Q2 is heat extracted from cold reservoir and W is work input."},{"id":1421,"text":"The reason of mirage in desert is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reflection"},{"label":"B","text":"Total internal reflection"},{"label":"C","text":"Diffraction"},{"label":"D","text":"Interference"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Mirage occurs due to total internal reflection of light in layers of different refractive indices."},{"id":1442,"text":"A body starts from rest and moves with uniform acceleration of 2 m/s². The distance covered in 5 seconds is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 m"},{"label":"B","text":"25 m"},{"label":"C","text":"50 m"},{"label":"D","text":"5 m"}],"answer":1,"solution":"s = ut + ½at² = 0 + ½ × 2 × 25 = 25 m."},{"id":1443,"text":"Which of the following has the highest specific heat capacity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron"},{"label":"B","text":"Copper"},{"label":"C","text":"Water"},{"label":"D","text":"Lead"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Water has the highest specific heat capacity (4200 J/kg°C) among common substances."},{"id":1444,"text":"The SI unit of electric potential is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coulomb"},{"label":"B","text":"Ohm"},{"label":"C","text":"Volt"},{"label":"D","text":"Ampere"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Electric potential is measured in Volts (V = J/C)."},{"id":1445,"text":"A convex mirror always forms which type of image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Real and inverted"},{"label":"B","text":"Virtual and erect"},{"label":"C","text":"Real and erect"},{"label":"D","text":"Virtual and inverted"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Convex mirrors always form virtual, erect, and diminished images regardless of object position."},{"id":1446,"text":"The dimensional formula of force is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"[MLT⁻¹]"},{"label":"B","text":"[ML⁻¹T⁻²]"},{"label":"C","text":"[MLT⁻²]"},{"label":"D","text":"[ML²T⁻²]"}],"answer":2,"solution":"F = ma ⇒ [F] = [M][LT⁻²] = [MLT⁻²]."},{"id":1457,"text":"The work done in moving a charge of 5 Cac ross two points with potential difference 10 V is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 J"},{"label":"B","text":"15 J"},{"label":"C","text":"50 J"},{"label":"D","text":"5 J"}],"answer":2,"solution":"W = QV = 5 × 10 = 50 J."},{"id":1458,"text":"The frequency of a wave with time period 0.02 s is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20 Hz"},{"label":"B","text":"50 Hz"},{"label":"C","text":"100 Hz"},{"label":"D","text":"25 Hz"}],"answer":1,"solution":"f = 1/T = 1/0.02 = 50 Hz."},{"id":1459,"text":"The refractive index of a medium is 1.5. Speed of light in this medium is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 × 10⁸ m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"2 × 10⁸ m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"4.5 × 10⁸ m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"1.5 × 10⁸ m/s"}],"answer":1,"solution":"v = c/n = 3×10⁸/1.5 = 2×10⁸ m/s."},{"id":1460,"text":"Which of the following is a scalar quantity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Force"},{"label":"B","text":"Velocity"},{"label":"C","text":"Energy"},{"label":"D","text":"Acceleration"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Energy has magnitude only (no direction), hence scalar. Force, velocity, acceleration are vectors."},{"id":1461,"text":"A body of mass 2 kg is moving with velocity 3 m/s. Its kinetic energy is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 J"},{"label":"B","text":"9 J"},{"label":"C","text":"6 J"},{"label":"D","text":"18 J"}],"answer":1,"solution":"KE = ½mv² = ½ × 2 × 9 = 9 J."},{"id":1472,"text":"A convex lens has a focal length of 20 cm. An object is placed at a distance of 30 cm from the lens. What is the nature and position of the image formed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Real, inverted, 60 cm from the lens on the opposite side."},{"label":"B","text":"Virtual, erect, 60 cm from the lens on the same side."},{"label":"C","text":"Real, inverted, 30 cm from the lens on the opposite side."},{"label":"D","text":"Virtual, erect, 30 cm from the lens on the same side."}],"answer":0,"solution":"Using the lens formula 1/f = 1/v - 1/u, where f = 20 cm and u = -30 cm (object distance is negative). 1/20 = 1/v - 1/(-30) => 1/v = 1/20 - 1/30 = (3-2)/60 = 1/60. So, v = 60 cm. Since v is positive, the image is real and formed on the opposite side. The magnification m = v/u = 60/(-30) = -2, indicating an inverted image."},{"id":1473,"text":"The direction of induced current is given by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampere's law"},{"label":"B","text":"Biot-Savart law"},{"label":"C","text":"Lenz's law"},{"label":"D","text":"Ohm's law"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Lenz's law determines direction: induced current opposes change in flux."},{"id":1475,"text":"A convex mirror always forms which type of image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Real and inverted"},{"label":"B","text":"Virtual and erect"},{"label":"C","text":"Real and erect"},{"label":"D","text":"Virtual and inverted"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Convex mirrors always form virtual, erect, and diminished images regardless of object position."},{"id":1476,"text":"A body of mass 2 kg is moving with velocity 3 m/s. Its kinetic energy is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 J"},{"label":"B","text":"9 J"},{"label":"C","text":"6 J"},{"label":"D","text":"18 J"}],"answer":1,"solution":"KE = ½mv² = ½ × 2 × 9 = 9 J."},{"id":1477,"text":"A wire of length Land area A has resistance R. If length is halved and area doubled, new resistance is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R/2"},{"label":"B","text":"R/4"},{"label":"C","text":"R"},{"label":"D","text":"2R"}],"answer":1,"solution":"R' = ρ(L/2)/(2A) = (ρL/A)/4 = R/4."},{"id":1478,"text":"A particle of mass m is released from rest at a height h above a horizontal surface. If it falls under gravity, and experiences a resistive force proportional to its velocity (F_r = -kv), where k is a positive constant, what is the terminal velocity it approaches?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"mg/k"},{"label":"B","text":"√(2gh)"},{"label":"C","text":"k/mg"},{"label":"D","text":"mgh/k"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Terminal velocity is reached when the net force on the particle is zero. The forces acting on the particle are gravity (mg downwards) and the resistive force (-kv upwards). Thus, mg - kv_t = 0, which gives v_t = mg/k."},{"id":1481,"text":"A thin uniform ring of mass Mand radius R is rotating about an axis perpendicular to its plane and passing through its center with an angular velocity ω. Two point masses, each of mass m, are gently attached at diametrically opposite points on the ring. The new angular velocity of the system will be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mω / (M + 2m)"},{"label":"B","text":"Mω / (M + m)"},{"label":"C","text":"(M + 2m)ω / M"},{"label":"D","text":"Mω / (M + m/2)"}],"answer":0,"solution":"According to the principle of conservation of angular momentum:Initial angular momentum (L_initial) = I_initial * ωFor the ring, I_initial = MR²So, L_initial = MR²ωWhen two point masses 'm' are attached at diametrically opposite points, the new moment of inertia (I_final) will be:I_final = I_ring + I_two_point_masses = MR² + mR² + mR² = (M + 2m)R²Let the new angular velocity be ω'.Final angular momentum (L_final) = I_final * ω' = (M + 2m)R²ω'Equating initial and final angular momentum:MR²ω = (M + 2m)R²ω'ω' = MR²ω / ((M + 2m)R²)ω' = Mω / (M + 2m)"},{"id":1482,"text":"A body starts from rest and moves with uniform acceleration of 2 m/s². The distance covered in 5 seconds is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 m"},{"label":"B","text":"25 m"},{"label":"C","text":"50 m"},{"label":"D","text":"5 m"}],"answer":1,"solution":"s = ut + ½at² = 0 + ½ × 2 × 25 = 25 m."},{"id":1490,"text":"Which of the following has the highest specific heat capacity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron"},{"label":"B","text":"Copper"},{"label":"C","text":"Water"},{"label":"D","text":"Lead"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Water has the highest specific heat capacity (4200 J/kg°C) among common substances."},{"id":1497,"text":"The internal resistance of an ideal voltmeter is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zero"},{"label":"B","text":"Low"},{"label":"C","text":"Infinity"},{"label":"D","text":"1 Ω"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Ideal voltmeter has infinite resistance to draw zero current."},{"id":1499,"text":"The reason of mirage in desert is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reflection"},{"label":"B","text":"Total internal reflection"},{"label":"C","text":"Diffraction"},{"label":"D","text":"Interference"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Mirage occurs due to total internal reflection of light in layers of different refractive indices."},{"id":1500,"text":"A solid cylinder of mass Mand radius R rolls without slipping on a horizontal surface with a linear velocity v. The kinetic energy of the cylinder is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(1/2)Mv^2"},{"label":"B","text":"(3/4)Mv^2"},{"label":"C","text":"Mv^2"},{"label":"D","text":"(1/4)Mv^2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The total kinetic energy (KE_total) of a body rolling without slipping is the sum of its translational kinetic energy (KE_translational) and rotational kinetic energy (KE_rotational).KE_total = KE_translational + KE_rotationalKE_translational = (1/2)Mv^2KE_rotational = (1/2)Iω^2For a solid cylinder, the moment of inertia I = (1/2)MR^2.For rolling without slipping, the linear velocity v and angular velocity ω are related by v = Rω, so ω = v/R.Substituting these into the rotational kinetic energy equation:KE_rotational = (1/2) * ((1/2)MR^2) * (v/R)^2KE_rotational = (1/4)MR^2 * (v^2/R^2)KE_rotational = (1/4)Mv^2Now, sum the translational and rotational kinetic energies:KE_total = (1/2)Mv^2 + (1/4)Mv^2KE_total = (2/4)Mv^2 + (1/4)Mv^2KE_total = (3/4)Mv^2"},{"id":1501,"text":"Which of the following is a scalar quantity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Force"},{"label":"B","text":"Velocity"},{"label":"C","text":"Energy"},{"label":"D","text":"Acceleration"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Energy has magnitude only (no direction), hence scalar. Force, velocity, acceleration are vectors."},{"id":1502,"text":"A body of mass 5 kg is moving with momentum 20 kg m/s. Its velocity is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"4 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"100 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"0.25 m/s"}],"answer":1,"solution":"v = p/m = 20/5 = 4 m/s."},{"id":1504,"text":"The de Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated through a potential difference V is given by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"h / sqrt(mV)"},{"label":"B","text":"h / sqrt(2meV)"},{"label":"C","text":"h / sqrt(2mVe)"},{"label":"D","text":"h / sqrt(V/2me)"}],"answer":1,"solution":"When an electron is accelerated through a potential difference V, it gains kinetic energy KE = eV. The kinetic energy is also given by KE = p^2 / 2m, where p is the momentum and m is the mass of the electron. Therefore, p = sqrt(2mKE) = sqrt(2meV). The de Broglie wavelength is given by  = h/p. Substituting the expression for p, we get  = h / sqrt(2meV)."},{"id":1509,"text":"The magnetic field at the center of a current carrying circular loop is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zero"},{"label":"B","text":"I/2R"},{"label":"C","text":"I/R"},{"label":"D","text":"I/4piR"}],"answer":1,"solution":"B = I/(2R) at center of circular loop."},{"id":1514,"text":"The energy equivalent of 1 amu is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"931 eV"},{"label":"B","text":"931 MeV"},{"label":"C","text":"931 keV"},{"label":"D","text":"931 J"}],"answer":1,"solution":"1 amu = 931.5 MeV/c^2."},{"id":1523,"text":"The internal resistance of an ideal voltmeter is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zero"},{"label":"B","text":"Low"},{"label":"C","text":"Infinity"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Ideal voltmeter has infinite resistance to draw zero current."},{"id":1525,"text":"Which of the following is a scalar quantity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Force"},{"label":"B","text":"Velocity"},{"label":"C","text":"Energy"},{"label":"D","text":"Acceleration"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Energy has magnitude only (no direction), hence scalar. Force, velocity, acceleration are vectors."},{"id":1528,"text":"The time period of a simple pendulum depends on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mass of bob"},{"label":"B","text":"Amplitude (small)"},{"label":"C","text":"Length of pendulum"},{"label":"D","text":"Material of bob"}],"answer":2,"solution":"T = 2pisqrt(L/g). Time period depends only on length and g."},{"id":1531,"text":"A convex mirror always forms which type of image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Real and inverted"},{"label":"B","text":"Virtual and erect"},{"label":"C","text":"Real and erect"},{"label":"D","text":"Virtual and inverted"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Convex mirrors always form virtual, erect, and diminished images regardless of object position."},{"id":1536,"text":"The time period of a simple pendulum depends on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mass of bob"},{"label":"B","text":"Amplitude (small)"},{"label":"C","text":"Length of pendulum"},{"label":"D","text":"Material of bob"}],"answer":2,"solution":"T = 2pisqrt(L/g). Time period depends only on length and g."},{"id":1537,"text":"Which of the following has the highest specific heat capacity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron"},{"label":"B","text":"Copper"},{"label":"C","text":"Water"},{"label":"D","text":"Lead"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Water has the highest specific heat capacity (4200 J/kg degrees C) among common substances."},{"id":1538,"text":"What is the nature of the force between two parallel current-carrying conductors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Repulsive"},{"label":"B","text":"Attractive"},{"label":"C","text":"Both attractive and repulsive"},{"label":"D","text":"Neutral"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The force between two parallel current-carrying conductors can be either attractive or repulsive, depending on the direction of the current flow."},{"id":1544,"text":"What is the phenomenon where an electric current is generated in a conductor when it is exposed to a changing magnetic field?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Electromagnetic induction"},{"label":"B","text":"Electrostatic induction"},{"label":"C","text":"Magnetic induction"},{"label":"D","text":"Electric induction"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Electromagnetic induction is the phenomenon where an electric current is generated in a conductor when it is exposed to a changing magnetic field."},{"id":1546,"text":"A force of 10 N acts on a body of mass 2 kgf or 5 s. The impulse is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 Ns"},{"label":"B","text":"50 Ns"},{"label":"C","text":"2 Ns"},{"label":"D","text":"25 Ns"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Impulse = F x t = 10 x 5 = 50 Ns."},{"id":1549,"text":"The refractive index of a medium is 1.5. Speed of light in this medium is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 x 10 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"2 x 10 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"4.5 x 10 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"1.5 x 10 m/s"}],"answer":1,"solution":"v = c/n = 3x10/1.5 = 2x10 m/s."},{"id":1550,"text":"A particle of mass m is released from rest at a height h above a horizontal surface. If it falls under gravity, and experiences a resistive force proportional to its velocity (F_r = -kv), where k is a positive constant, what is the terminal velocity it approaches?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"mg/k"},{"label":"B","text":"sqrt(2gh)"},{"label":"C","text":"k/mg"},{"label":"D","text":"mgh/k"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Terminal velocity is reached when the net force on the particle is zero. The forces acting on the particle are gravity (mg downwards) and the resistive force (-kv upwards). Thus, mg - kv_t = 0, which gives v_t = mg/k."},{"id":1551,"text":"The dimension of Planck's constant is same as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Energy"},{"label":"B","text":"Angular momentum"},{"label":"C","text":"Force"},{"label":"D","text":"Power"}],"answer":1,"solution":"h = E/ -> [ML^2T] same as angular momentum."},{"id":1558,"text":"The de Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated through a potential difference V is given by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"h / sqrt(mV)"},{"label":"B","text":"h / sqrt(2meV)"},{"label":"C","text":"h / sqrt(2mVe)"},{"label":"D","text":"h / sqrt(V/2me)"}],"answer":1,"solution":"When an electron is accelerated through a potential difference V, it gains kinetic energy KE = eV. The kinetic energy is also given by KE = p^2 / 2m, where p is the momentum and m is the mass of the electron. Therefore, p = sqrt(2mKE) = sqrt(2meV). The de Broglie wavelength is given by  = h/p. Substituting the expression for p, we get  = h / sqrt(2meV)."},{"id":1559,"text":"The energy equivalent of 1 amu is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"931 eV"},{"label":"B","text":"931 MeV"},{"label":"C","text":"931 keV"},{"label":"D","text":"931 J"}],"answer":1,"solution":"1 amu = 931.5 MeV/c^2."},{"id":1565,"text":"What is the phenomenon where an electric current is generated in a conductor when it is exposed to a changing magnetic field?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Electromagnetic induction"},{"label":"B","text":"Electrostatic induction"},{"label":"C","text":"Magnetic induction"},{"label":"D","text":"Electric induction"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Electromagnetic induction is the phenomenon where an electric current is generated in a conductor when it is exposed to a changing magnetic field."},{"id":1569,"text":"An electron is accelerated through a potential difference V. If its de Broglie wavelength is , then the de Broglie wavelength of a proton accelerated through the same potential difference V will be: (Given: m_p is the mass of proton, m_e is the mass of electron)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"* sqrt(m_e / m_p)"},{"label":"B","text":"* sqrt(m_p / m_e)"},{"label":"C","text":"* (m_e / m_p)"},{"label":"D","text":"* (m_p / m_e)"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The de Broglie wavelength () is given by  = h/p, where h is Planck's constant and p is the momentum. When a charged particle (charge q, mass m) is accelerated through a potential difference V, its kinetic energy (KE) is qV.\r\nAlso, KE = p^2 / (2m), so p = sqrt(2mKE) = sqrt(2mqV).\r\nTherefore, the de Broglie wavelength can be written as  = h / sqrt(2mqV).\r\nFor an electron:\r\n_e = h / sqrt(2m_e q_e V) =  (given)\r\nFor a proton:\r\n_p = h / sqrt(2m_p q_p V)\r\nSince the charge of an electron (q_e) and a proton (q_p) are equal in magnitude (let's denote it as e), and they are accelerated through the same potential V:\r\n_e = h / sqrt(2m_e e V)\r\n_p = h / sqrt(2m_p e V)\r\nNow, let's find the ratio _p / _e:\r\n_p / _e = [h / sqrt(2m_p e V)] / [h / sqrt(2m_e e V)]\r\n_p / _e = sqrt(2m_e e V) / sqrt(2m_p e V)\r\n_p / _e = sqrt(m_e / m_p)\r\nSo, _p =  * sqrt(m_e / m_p)."},{"id":1570,"text":"A uniform rod of length Land mass M is pivoted at one end. It is released from a horizontal position. What is the angular acceleration of the rod when it is vertical?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3g/L"},{"label":"B","text":"2g/L"},{"label":"C","text":"3g/2L"},{"label":"D","text":"g/L"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The moment of inertia of a rod about one end is I = (1/3)ML^2. The torque due to gravity when the rod is vertical is  = Mg(L/2). Using Newton'sse cond law for rotation,  = I, we get Mg(L/2) = (1/3)ML^2. Solving for , we get  = 3g/2L."},{"id":1573,"text":"A convex lens has a focal length of 20 cm. An object is placed at a distance of 30 cm from the lens. What is the nature and position of the image formed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Real, inverted, 60 cm from the lens on the opposite side."},{"label":"B","text":"Virtual, erect, 60 cm from the lens on the same side."},{"label":"C","text":"Real, inverted, 30 cm from the lens on the opposite side."},{"label":"D","text":"Virtual, erect, 30 cm from the lens on the same side."}],"answer":0,"solution":"Using the lens formula 1/f = 1/v - 1/u, where f = 20 cm and u = -30 cm (object distance is negative). 1/20 = 1/v - 1/(-30) => 1/v = 1/20 - 1/30 = (3-2)/60 = 1/60. So, v = 60 cm. Since v is positive, the image is real and formed on the opposite side. The magnification m = v/u = 60/(-30) = -2, indicating an inverted image."},{"id":1576,"text":"The direction of induced current is given by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampere's law"},{"label":"B","text":"Biot-Savart law"},{"label":"C","text":"Lenz's law"},{"label":"D","text":"Ohm's law"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Lenz's law determines direction: induced current opposes change in flux."},{"id":1579,"text":"What is the nature of the force between two parallel current-carrying conductors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Repulsive"},{"label":"B","text":"Attractive"},{"label":"C","text":"Both attractive and repulsive"},{"label":"D","text":"Neutral"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The force between two parallel current-carrying conductors can be either attractive or repulsive, depending on the direction of the current flow."},{"id":1585,"text":"A solid cylinder of mass Mand radius R rolls without slipping on a horizontal surface with a linear velocity v. The kinetic energy of the cylinder is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(1/2)Mv^2"},{"label":"B","text":"(3/4)Mv^2"},{"label":"C","text":"Mv^2"},{"label":"D","text":"(1/4)Mv^2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The total kinetic energy (KE_total) of a body rolling without slipping is the sum of its translational kinetic energy (KE_translational) and rotational kinetic energy (KE_rotational).\r\nKE_total = KE_translational + KE_rotational\r\nKE_translational = (1/2)Mv^2\r\nKE_rotational = (1/2)I^2\r\nFor a solid cylinder, the moment of inertia I = (1/2)MR^2.\r\nFor rolling without slipping, the linear velocity v and angular velocity  are related by v = R, so  = v/R.\r\nSubstituting these into the rotational kinetic energy equation:\r\nKE_rotational = (1/2) * ((1/2)MR^2) * (v/R)^2\r\nKE_rotational = (1/4)MR^2 * (v^2/R^2)\r\nKE_rotational = (1/4)Mv^2\r\nNow, sum the translational and rotational kinetic energies:\r\nKE_total = (1/2)Mv^2 + (1/4)Mv^2\r\nKE_total = (2/4)Mv^2 + (1/4)Mv^2\r\nKE_total = (3/4)Mv^2"},{"id":1587,"text":"A body of mass 5 kg is moving with momentum 20 kg m/s. Its velocity is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"4 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"100 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"0.25 m/s"}],"answer":1,"solution":"v = p/m = 20/5 = 4 m/s."},{"id":1594,"text":"What is the unit of measurement for entropy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Joule"},{"label":"B","text":"Joule per Kelvin"},{"label":"C","text":"Kelvin"},{"label":"D","text":"Newton"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Entropy is measured in units of Joule per Kelvin (J/K)."},{"id":1595,"text":"The reason of mirage in desert is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reflection"},{"label":"B","text":"Total internal reflection"},{"label":"C","text":"Diffraction"},{"label":"D","text":"Interference"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Mirage occurs due to total internal reflection of light in layers of different refractive indices."},{"id":1599,"text":"A wire of length Land area A has resistance R. If length is halved and area doubled, new resistance is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R/2"},{"label":"B","text":"R/4"},{"label":"C","text":"R"},{"label":"D","text":"2R"}],"answer":1,"solution":"R' = (L/2)/(2A) = (L/A)/4 = R/4."},{"id":1600,"text":"The work done in moving a charge of 5 Cac ross two points with potential difference 10 V is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 J"},{"label":"B","text":"15 J"},{"label":"C","text":"50 J"},{"label":"D","text":"5 J"}],"answer":2,"solution":"W = QV = 5 x 10 = 50 J."},{"id":1603,"text":"Which of the following has the highest specific heat capacity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron"},{"label":"B","text":"Copper"},{"label":"C","text":"Water"},{"label":"D","text":"Lead"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Water has the highest specific heat capacity (4200 J/kg degrees C) among common substances."},{"id":1614,"text":"The SI unit of electric potential is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coulomb"},{"label":"B","text":"Ohm"},{"label":"C","text":"Volt"},{"label":"D","text":"Ampere"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Electric potential is measured in Volts (V = J/C)."},{"id":1615,"text":"What is the phenomenon where an electric current generates a magnetic field?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Electromagnetic induction"},{"label":"B","text":"Magnetic induction"},{"label":"C","text":"Ampere's law"},{"label":"D","text":"Faraday's law"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Ampere's law states that an electric current generates a magnetic field, and the direction of the magnetic field can be determined by the right-hand rule."},{"id":1620,"text":"Which law states that entropy of an isolated system always increases?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"First law"},{"label":"B","text":"Second law"},{"label":"C","text":"Third law"},{"label":"D","text":"Zeroth law"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Second law: entropy of isolated system always increases (S  0)."},{"id":1621,"text":"A thin uniform ring of mass Mand radius R is rotating about an axis perpendicular to its plane and passing through its center with an angular velocity . Two point masses, each of mass m, are gently attached at diametrically opposite points on the ring. The new angular velocity of the system will be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"M / (M + 2m)"},{"label":"B","text":"M / (M + m)"},{"label":"C","text":"(M + 2m) / M"},{"label":"D","text":"M / (M + m/2)"}],"answer":0,"solution":"According to the principle of conservation of angular momentum:\r\nInitial angular momentum (L_initial) = I_initial * \r\nFor the ring, I_initial = MR^2\r\nSo, L_initial = MR^2\r\n\r\nWhen two point masses 'm' are attached at diametrically opposite points, the new moment of inertia (I_final) will be:\r\nI_final = I_ring + I_two_point_masses = MR^2 + mR^2 + mR^2 = (M + 2m)R^2\r\n\r\nLet the new angular velocity be '.\r\nFinal angular momentum (L_final) = I_final * ' = (M + 2m)R^2'\r\n\r\nEquating initial and final angular momentum:\r\nMR^2 = (M + 2m)R^2'\r\n' = MR^2 / ((M + 2m)R^2)\r\n' = M / (M + 2m)"},{"id":1624,"text":"A wire of length Land area A has resistance R. If length is halved and area doubled, new resistance is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R/2"},{"label":"B","text":"R/4"},{"label":"C","text":"R"},{"label":"D","text":"2R"}],"answer":1,"solution":"R' = (L/2)/(2A) = (L/A)/4 = R/4."},{"id":1627,"text":"A convex mirror always forms which type of image?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Real and inverted"},{"label":"B","text":"Virtual and erect"},{"label":"C","text":"Real and erect"},{"label":"D","text":"Virtual and inverted"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Convex mirrors always form virtual, erect, and diminished images regardless of object position."},{"id":1628,"text":"What is the phenomenon where the frequency of a wave appears to change when the source and observer are moving relative to each other?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diffraction"},{"label":"B","text":"Refraction"},{"label":"C","text":"Interference"},{"label":"D","text":"Doppler effect"}],"answer":3,"solution":"The Doppler effect is the phenomenon where the frequency of a wave appears to change when the source and observer are moving relative to each other. This effect is commonly observed in sound waves and light waves."},{"id":1634,"text":"A convex lens has a focal length of 20 cm. An object is placed at a distance of 30 cm from the lens. What is the nature and position of the image formed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Real, inverted, 60 cm from the lens on the opposite side."},{"label":"B","text":"Virtual, erect, 60 cm from the lens on the same side."},{"label":"C","text":"Real, inverted, 30 cm from the lens on the opposite side."},{"label":"D","text":"Virtual, erect, 30 cm from the lens on the same side."}],"answer":0,"solution":"Using the lens formula 1/f = 1/v - 1/u, where f = 20 cm and u = -30 cm (object distance is negative). 1/20 = 1/v - 1/(-30) => 1/v = 1/20 - 1/30 = (3-2)/60 = 1/60. So, v = 60 cm. Since v is positive, the image is real and formed on the opposite side. The magnification m = v/u = 60/(-30) = -2, indicating an inverted image."},{"id":1637,"text":"What is the term for the ability of a material to conduct heat?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thermal conductivity"},{"label":"B","text":"Thermal resistance"},{"label":"C","text":"Specific heat capacity"},{"label":"D","text":"Thermal energy"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Thermal conductivity is the ability of a material to conduct heat. It is measured in units of watts per meter per kelvin (W/mK) and is an important property of materials in various engineering applications."},{"id":1641,"text":"What is the phenomenon where an electric current generates a magnetic field?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Electromagnetic induction"},{"label":"B","text":"Magnetic induction"},{"label":"C","text":"Ampere's law"},{"label":"D","text":"Faraday's law"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Ampere's law states that an electric current generates a magnetic field, and the direction of the magnetic field can be determined by the right-hand rule."},{"id":1643,"text":"The SI unit of electric potential is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coulomb"},{"label":"B","text":"Ohm"},{"label":"C","text":"Volt"},{"label":"D","text":"Ampere"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Electric potential is measured in Volts (V = J/C)."},{"id":1650,"text":"A solid cylinder of mass Mand radius R rolls without slipping on a horizontal surface with a linear velocity v. The kinetic energy of the cylinder is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(1/2)Mv^2"},{"label":"B","text":"(3/4)Mv^2"},{"label":"C","text":"Mv^2"},{"label":"D","text":"(1/4)Mv^2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The total kinetic energy (KE_total) of a body rolling without slipping is the sum of its translational kinetic energy (KE_translational) and rotational kinetic energy (KE_rotational).\r\nKE_total = KE_translational + KE_rotational\r\nKE_translational = (1/2)Mv^2\r\nKE_rotational = (1/2)I^2\r\nFor a solid cylinder, the moment of inertia I = (1/2)MR^2.\r\nFor rolling without slipping, the linear velocity v and angular velocity  are related by v = R, so  = v/R.\r\nSubstituting these into the rotational kinetic energy equation:\r\nKE_rotational = (1/2) * ((1/2)MR^2) * (v/R)^2\r\nKE_rotational = (1/4)MR^2 * (v^2/R^2)\r\nKE_rotational = (1/4)Mv^2\r\nNow, sum the translational and rotational kinetic energies:\r\nKE_total = (1/2)Mv^2 + (1/4)Mv^2\r\nKE_total = (2/4)Mv^2 + (1/4)Mv^2\r\nKE_total = (3/4)Mv^2"},{"id":1673,"text":"In Young's double slit experiment, the fringe width is β. If the entire apparatus is immersed in a liquid of refractive index μ = 1.5, the new fringe width becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.5 β"},{"label":"B","text":"β / 1.5"},{"label":"C","text":"β"},{"label":"D","text":"2.25 β"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Fringe width β ∝ λ; in a medium λ' = λ/μ, so β' = β/μ = β/1.5. Full Solution: The fringe width formula is β = λD/d. Immersing the apparatus in liquid reduces the wavelength to λ/μ while D and d remain unchanged, resulting in a new fringe width β' = β/μ."},{"id":1674,"text":"The radius of the first Bohr orbit of hydrogen atom is a₀. The radius of the second orbit (n=2) of Li²⁺ ion (Z=3) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(4/3) a₀"},{"label":"B","text":"(3/4) a₀"},{"label":"C","text":"(2/3) a₀"},{"label":"D","text":"(9/4) a₀"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Bohr radius r_n ∝ n²/Z. For H (n=1, Z=1) r=a₀. For Li²⁺ (n=2, Z=3) r = a₀ × (2²/3) / (1²/1) = 4a₀/3. Full Solution: Using the formula r_n = (n²/Z) a₀, substituting n=2 and Z=3 for Li²⁺ gives r = (4/3) a₀."},{"id":1675,"text":"A series LCR circuit has a resonant frequency ω₀ = 1000 rad/s and a quality factor Q = 50. The bandwidth (Δω) of the circuit is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 rad/s"},{"label":"B","text":"200 rad/s"},{"label":"C","text":"50000 rad/s"},{"label":"D","text":"20 rad/s"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Bandwidth Δω = ω₀/Q = 1000/50 = 20 rad/s. Full Solution: By definition, the quality factor for a series resonant circuit is Q = ω₀/Δω. Rearranging gives Δω = ω₀/Q = 1000/50 = 20 rad/s."},{"id":1687,"text":"In a photoelectric effect experiment, the frequency of incident light is kept constant while the intensity is doubled. What is the effect on the stopping potential (V_s) and the saturation photocurrent (I_s)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"V_s doubles, I_s doubles"},{"label":"B","text":"V_sre mains same, I_s doubles"},{"label":"C","text":"V_s halves, I_sre mains same"},{"label":"D","text":"V_sre mains same, I_sre mains same"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Stopping potential depends only on frequency (Einstein's equation: eV_s = hν - φ), so it is unchanged; saturation current is directly proportional to intensity (photon flux), so it doubles. Full Solution: Since frequency ν is constant, the maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons (eV_s) remains constant, leaving V_sun changed. Doubling intensity doubles the number of incident photons per second, doubling the photoelectron emission rate and hence the saturation current I_s."},{"id":1688,"text":"A parallel plate capacitor is charged to a potential difference Vand then disconnected from the battery. A dielectric slab of dielectric constant K is inserted between the plates, completely filling the space. The new energy stored in the capacitor becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Initial energy / K^2"},{"label":"B","text":"K times initial energy"},{"label":"C","text":"Initial energy / K"},{"label":"D","text":"K^2 times initial energy"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: With battery disconnected, charge Q is constant; energy U = Q²/2C. Capacitance increases K times (C' = KC), so energy decreases K times (U' = U/K). Full Solution: Initial energy U = Q²/(2C₀). After inserting dielectric, C = KC₀. Since Q is constant, final energy U' = Q²/(2KC₀) = U/K."},{"id":1689,"text":"A satellite orbits very close to the Earth's surface. The ratio of its escape velocity (v_e) to its orbital velocity (v_o) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 : 1"},{"label":"B","text":"1 : 1"},{"label":"C","text":"1 : √2"},{"label":"D","text":"√2 : 1"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Orbital velocity v_o = √(GM/R); Escape velocity v_e = √(2GM/R) = √2 v_o. Ratio v_e : v_o = √2 : 1. Full Solution: For a satellite near surface, orbital radius ≈ R. Centripetal force: mv_o²/R = GMm/R² ⇒ v_o = √(GM/R). Escape velocity from energy conservation: ½mv_e² = GMm/R ⇒ v_e = √(2GM/R) = √2 v_o."},{"id":1690,"text":"For a transistor in common-base configuration, the current gain α is 0.98. The current gain β in common-emitter configuration is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"49"},{"label":"B","text":"98"},{"label":"C","text":"0.02"},{"label":"D","text":"50"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: β = α/(1-α). Here 1-α = 0.02, so β = 0.98/0.02 = 49. Full Solution: The relation between α (common-base current gain) and β (common-emitter current gain) is β = α/(1-α). Substituting α = 0.98 gives β = 0.98 / (1 - 0.98) = 0.98 / 0.02 = 49."},{"id":1704,"text":"In a potentiometer experiment, the null point for a test cell of emf E is found at 75 cm. If the emf of the driver cell is reduced to half its original value while the potentiometer wire resistance remains unchanged, the new null point length will be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"37.5 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"75 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"112.5 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"150 cm"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Potential gradient k ∝ V_driver. At null point, E = k·l ⇒ l ∝ 1/k ∝ 1/V_driver. Halving V_driver doubles the null length. 75 cm × 2 = 150 cm. Full Solution: Initially E = k₁l₁ where k₁ = V₁/R_wire (assuming series resistance negligible or constant). New gradient k₂ = (V₁/2)/R_wire = k₁/2. New length l₂ = E/k₂ = E/(k₁/2) = 2(E/k₁) = 2l₁ = 150 cm."},{"id":1705,"text":"An electron and a proton are accelerated from rest through the same potential difference V. The ratio of their de Broglie wavelengths (λₑ : λₚ) is approximately: (Given mₚ ≈ 1836 mₑ)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"√1836 : 1"},{"label":"B","text":"1 : √1836"},{"label":"C","text":"1836 : 1"},{"label":"D","text":"1 : 1836"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Kinetic energy K = qV is same for both (|q|=e). de Broglie wavelength λ = h/√(2mK) ∝ 1/√m. Ratio λₑ/λₚ = √(mₚ/mₑ) = √1836. Full Solution: Kₑ = eV, Kₚ = eV. λ = h/p = h/√(2mK). λₑ = h/√(2mₑeV), λₚ = h/√(2mₚeV). λₑ/λₚ = √(mₚ/mₑ) = √1836 ≈ 42.85. Thus ratio is √1836 : 1."},{"id":1717,"text":"A circular loop of radius R carries a current I. The magnetic field at its centre is B. If the current is doubled and the radius is halved, the new magnetic field at the centre becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4B"},{"label":"B","text":"2B"},{"label":"C","text":"8B"},{"label":"D","text":"B/2"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Magnetic field at centre of a loop is proportional to I/R. Doubling I and halving R multiplies B by 2/(1/2)=4. Full solution: B = μ₀I/(2R). New B = μ₀(2I)/(2(R/2)) = μ₀(2I)/R = 4 × μ₀I/(2R) = 4B."},{"id":1718,"text":"A steel wire of length 2 m and cross‑sectional area 1 mm² is stretched by a load of 100 N. If Young’s modulus of steel is 2×10¹¹ N/m², the elastic potential energy stored in the wire is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 J"},{"label":"B","text":"0.05 J"},{"label":"C","text":"5 J"},{"label":"D","text":"50 J"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Use U = F²L/(2AY); plugging F=100 N, L=2 m, A=1×10⁻⁶ m², Y=2×10¹¹ N/m² gives U=0.05 J. Full solution: The work done in stretching is ∫F dx = ½Fx; using Hooke's law F = (AY/L)x, we get U = ½ (AY/L) x² = F²L/(2AY) = 0.05 J."},{"id":1719,"text":"In a series RL circuit, the resistance is 3 Ω and the inductive reactance is 4 Ω. The power factor of the circuit is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.6"},{"label":"B","text":"0.8"},{"label":"C","text":"0.5"},{"label":"D","text":"1.0"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: For a series RL circuit, power factor = R/√(R²+X_L²); with R=3 Ω, X_L=4 Ω, Z=5 Ω, so cos φ = 3/5 = 0.6. Full solution: Impedance Z = √(3²+4²) = 5 Ω; power factor = R/Z = 3/5 = 0.6."},{"id":1732,"text":"A Carnot engine operates between a hot reservoir at 500 K and a cold reservoir at 300 K. If the temperature of the cold reservoir is increased by 50 Kwh ile the hot reservoir temperature remains unchanged, the new efficiency of the engine becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.30"},{"label":"B","text":"0.35"},{"label":"C","text":"0.40"},{"label":"D","text":"0.45"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Efficiency η = 1 - Tc/Th. New Tc = 350 K, Th = 500 K → η = 1 - 350/500 = 150/500 = 0.30. Step-by-step: Original η = 1 - 300/500 = 0.40. After increase, Tc = 350 K, so η_new = (500-350)/500 = 150/500 = 0.30."},{"id":1733,"text":"A convex lens of focal length 15 cm forms a real image of an object placed at 30 cm from the lens. The image distance from the lens is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"30 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"20 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"15 cm"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Use lens formula 1/f = 1/v + 1/u. For f=15 cm, u=30 cm → 1/v = 1/15 - 1/30 = 1/30 → v = 30 cm. Step-by-step: 1/15 = 1/v + 1/30 ⇒ 1/v = 2/30 - 1/30 = 1/30 ⇒ v = 30 cm."},{"id":1734,"text":"The electric field at a point on the axial line of a short dipole at distance r from its centre is E. If the distance is doubled to 2r, the electric field becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E/2"},{"label":"B","text":"E/4"},{"label":"C","text":"E/8"},{"label":"D","text":"E/16"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Axial field of a dipole varies as 1/r³. Doubling r reduces field by factor 2³ = 8 → E_new = E/8. Step-by-step: E ∝ 1/r³ ⇒ E_new/E = (r/(2r))³ = (1/2)³ = 1/8 ⇒ E_new = E/8."},{"id":1747,"text":"In a series LCR circuit, the resonant frequency is f₀. If the inductance is doubled and the capacitance is halved, the new resonant frequency becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"f₀"},{"label":"B","text":"2f₀"},{"label":"C","text":"f₀/√2"},{"label":"D","text":"f₀/2"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Resonant frequency f ∝ 1/√(LC); doubling Land halving Cleaves the product LC unchanged, so f remains f₀. Full solution: f₀ = 1/(2π√(LC)). With L' = 2Land C' = C/2, L'C' = (2L)(C/2) = LC, hence f' = 1/(2π√(L'C')) = 1/(2π√(LC)) = f₀."},{"id":1749,"text":"According to the Bohr model, the kinetic energy of the electron in the nth orbit of hydrogen is K_n. The ratio K₂ : K₃ is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4/9"},{"label":"B","text":"2/3"},{"label":"C","text":"9/4"},{"label":"D","text":"3/2"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: In Bohr model K_n ∝ 1/n²; thus K₂/K₃ = (1/2²)/(1/3²) = (1/4)/(1/9) = 9/4. Full solution: K_n = (13.6 eV)/n². So K₂ = 13.6/4 = 3.4 eV, K₃ = 13.6/9 ≈ 1.51 eV. Ratio K₂/K₃ = (13.6/4)/(13.6/9) = 9/4."},{"id":1762,"text":"In a Young's double slit experiment, the fringe width is β. If the wavelength of light used is doubled, the separation between the slits is halved, and the distance between the slits and the screen is tripled, the new fringe width becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3β"},{"label":"B","text":"6β"},{"label":"C","text":"12β"},{"label":"D","text":"24β"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Fringe width β ∝ λD/d. Multiply the factors of change: (2 for λ) × (3 for D) ÷ (0.5 for d) = 12. New β = 12β. Full Solution: Original β = λD/d. New β' = (2λ)(3D)/(d/2) = 12(λD/d) = 12β."},{"id":1763,"text":"Light of frequency 1.5ν₀ is incident on a photosensitive metal with threshold frequency ν₀. The stopping potential is measured as V₀. If the frequency of incident light is changed to 2.5ν₀, the new stopping potential will be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2V₀"},{"label":"B","text":"3V₀"},{"label":"C","text":"4V₀"},{"label":"D","text":"1.5V₀"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Einstein's equation eVₛ = h(ν - ν₀). Stopping potential is proportional to (ν - ν₀). Ratio = (2.5ν₀ - ν₀) / (1.5ν₀ - ν₀) = 1.5ν₀ / 0.5ν₀ = 3. New Vₛ = 3V₀. Full Solution: eV₀ = h(0.5ν₀). For ν = 2.5ν₀, eVₛ' = h(1.5ν₀) = 3 × h(0.5ν₀) = 3eV₀ ⇒ Vₛ' = 3V₀."},{"id":1764,"text":"A conducting rod of length l rotates with a constant angular velocity ω in a uniform magnetic field B directed perpendicular to the plane of rotation. One end of the rod is fixed at the centre of rotation. The magnitude of the induced emf between the two ends of the rod is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"½ Bωl²"},{"label":"B","text":"Bωl²"},{"label":"C","text":"¼ Bωl²"},{"label":"D","text":"2 Bωl²"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Emf = Rate of magnetic flux cutting = B × (Area swept per second). Area swept per second = ½ ωl². Hence ε = ½ Bωl². Full Solution: Consider an element dr at distance r. Velocity v = ωr. dε = B v dr = Bωr dr. Integrating from 0 to l: ε = Bω ∫₀ˡ r dr = Bω [r²/2]₀ˡ = ½ Bωl²."},{"id":1765,"text":"A uniform disc rolls without slipping on a horizontal surface with a centre of mass speed v. The speed of the topmost point of the disc relative to the ground is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"v"},{"label":"B","text":"√2 v"},{"label":"C","text":"0"},{"label":"D","text":"2v"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Pure rolling condition v = Rω. Velocity of topmost point = v_cm + Rω (same direction) = v + v = 2v. Full Solution: For pure rolling, angular speed ω = v/R. Velocity of any point is vector sum of v_cm and ω × r. At the top, r = R upward, ω × r is horizontal forward with magnitude Rω = v. Net speed = v + v = 2v."}]},{"name":"Biology","count":79,"questions":[{"id":1402,"text":"Which organelle is involved in protein synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitochondria"},{"label":"B","text":"Ribosome"},{"label":"C","text":"Lysosome"},{"label":"D","text":"Golgi body"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Ribosomes (composed of rRNA + proteins) synthesize proteins."},{"id":1403,"text":"Which disease is caused by a virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cholera"},{"label":"B","text":"Typhoid"},{"label":"C","text":"AIDS"},{"label":"D","text":"Malaria"}],"answer":2,"solution":"AIDS is caused by HIV (virus). Cholera/typhoid: bacteria, Malaria: protozoa."},{"id":1404,"text":"The process of cell division for growth is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meiosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Mitosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Binary fission"},{"label":"D","text":"Budding"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Mitosis produces identical daughter cells for growth and repair."},{"id":1405,"text":"The enzyme that breaks down proteins in stomach is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amylase"},{"label":"B","text":"Pepsin"},{"label":"C","text":"Lipase"},{"label":"D","text":"Trypsin"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Pepsin (in gastric juice) breaks proteins into peptides in acidic medium."},{"id":1406,"text":"Which part of the flower becomes fruit?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Petal"},{"label":"B","text":"Sepal"},{"label":"C","text":"Ovary"},{"label":"D","text":"Stamen"}],"answer":2,"solution":"After fertilization, the ovary develops into the fruit."},{"id":1407,"text":"The main function of RBC is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood clotting"},{"label":"B","text":"Oxygen transport"},{"label":"C","text":"Immunity"},{"label":"D","text":"Antibody production"}],"answer":1,"solution":"RBCs (erythrocytes) contain hemoglobin to transport O2 and CO2."},{"id":1408,"text":"Which hormone is called the \"fight or flight\" hormone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insulin"},{"label":"B","text":"Adrenaline"},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroxine"},{"label":"D","text":"Estrogen"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Adrenaline (epinephrine) prepares body for stress (increases HR, BP, etc.)."},{"id":1409,"text":"The smallest bone in the human body is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Femur"},{"label":"B","text":"Stapes"},{"label":"C","text":"Patella"},{"label":"D","text":"Phalanges"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Stapes (stirrup bone in middle ear) is the smallest bone (~3 mm)."},{"id":1410,"text":"Which of these is a vestigial organ in humans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liver"},{"label":"B","text":"Heart"},{"label":"C","text":"Appendix"},{"label":"D","text":"Kidney"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Appendix is a vestigial organ (remnant of larger cecum in herbivore ancestors)."},{"id":1411,"text":"The green pigment in plants that traps sunlight is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Xanthophyll"},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorophyll"},{"label":"C","text":"Carotene"},{"label":"D","text":"Phycobilin"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Chlorophyll absorbs sunlight (mainly blue and red) for photosynthesis."},{"id":1432,"text":"Which vitamin is water soluble?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin A"},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin D"},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin C"},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin K"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Vitamin Cand B-complex are water-soluble. A, D, E, K aref at-soluble."},{"id":1433,"text":"The largest gland in the human body is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancreas"},{"label":"B","text":"Liver"},{"label":"C","text":"Salivary gland"},{"label":"D","text":"Thyroid"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Liver is the largest gland (~1.5 kg) and performs 500+ functions."},{"id":1434,"text":"Which of these is a prokaryote?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amoeba"},{"label":"B","text":"Bacteria"},{"label":"C","text":"Yeast"},{"label":"D","text":"Paramecium"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Bacteria are prokaryotes (no membrane-bound nucleus/organelles)."},{"id":1435,"text":"The universal donor blood group is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"AB"},{"label":"D","text":"O"}],"answer":3,"solution":"O negative is universal donor (no A/B antigens, no Rh factor)."},{"id":1436,"text":"The respiratory pigment in human blood is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlorophyll"},{"label":"B","text":"Hemoglobin"},{"label":"C","text":"Melanin"},{"label":"D","text":"Myoglobin"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Hemoglobin in RBCs transports O2 from lungs to tissues."},{"id":1437,"text":"Which part of the brain controls balance and coordination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cerebrum"},{"label":"B","text":"Cerebellum"},{"label":"C","text":"Medulla"},{"label":"D","text":"Hypothalamus"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Cerebellum coordinates voluntary movements, balance, and posture."},{"id":1438,"text":"The number of chromosomes in human cells is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"23"},{"label":"B","text":"46"},{"label":"C","text":"48"},{"label":"D","text":"44"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Humans have 23 pairs = 46 chromosomes (22 autosomes + 1 sex chromosome pair)."},{"id":1439,"text":"The disease caused by deficiency of insulin is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Goiter"},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetes mellitus"},{"label":"C","text":"Anemia"},{"label":"D","text":"Rickets"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Diabetes mellitus is caused by insufficient insulin production or insulin resistance."},{"id":1440,"text":"The DNA structure was discovered by:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mendel"},{"label":"B","text":"Darwin"},{"label":"C","text":"Watson and Crick"},{"label":"D","text":"Pasteur"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Watson and Crick proposed the double helix structure of DNA in 1953."},{"id":1441,"text":"Which chamber of the heart pumps blood to the lungs?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Left atrium"},{"label":"B","text":"Left ventricle"},{"label":"C","text":"Right atrium"},{"label":"D","text":"Right ventricle"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Right ventricle pumps deoxygenated blood to lungs via pulmonary artery."},{"id":1452,"text":"The powerhouse of the cell is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nucleus"},{"label":"B","text":"Ribosome"},{"label":"C","text":"Mitochondria"},{"label":"D","text":"Golgi apparatus"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Mitochondria produce ATP through cellular respiration, hence called the powerhouse of the cell."},{"id":1453,"text":"Which vitamin is produced in the skin on exposure to sunlight?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin A"},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin D"},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin C"},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin B12"}],"answer":1,"solution":"UV rays convert 7-dehydrocholesterol to Vitamin D3 in the skin."},{"id":1454,"text":"The human heart has how many chambers?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Human heart has 4 chambers: 2 atria and 2 ventricles."},{"id":1455,"text":"DNA replication is which type of process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conservative"},{"label":"B","text":"Semiconservative"},{"label":"C","text":"Dispersive"},{"label":"D","text":"Random"}],"answer":1,"solution":"DNA replication follows the semiconservative model as demonstrated by Meselson and Stahl."},{"id":1456,"text":"Which hormone regulates blood sugar level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroxine"},{"label":"B","text":"Insulin"},{"label":"C","text":"Adrenaline"},{"label":"D","text":"Estrogen"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Insulin lowers blood glucose, while glucagon raises it."},{"id":1467,"text":"The longest bone in the human body is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Humerus"},{"label":"B","text":"Femur"},{"label":"C","text":"Tibia"},{"label":"D","text":"Fibula"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Femur (thigh bone) is the longest and strongest bone in the human body."},{"id":1468,"text":"Which organ is responsible for blood filtration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liver"},{"label":"B","text":"Kidney"},{"label":"C","text":"Heart"},{"label":"D","text":"Lungs"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Kidneys filter blood through nephrons, removing waste and excess water to form urine."},{"id":1469,"text":"The pigment responsible for skin color is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlorophyll"},{"label":"B","text":"Haemoglobin"},{"label":"C","text":"Melanin"},{"label":"D","text":"Carotene"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Melanin produced by melanocytes determines skin, hair, and eye color."},{"id":1470,"text":"Blood group with no antigens on RBC is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"AB"},{"label":"D","text":"O"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Blood group O has no A or B antigens on RBCs but has both anti-A and anti-B antibodies."},{"id":1471,"text":"Which enzyme breaks down starch in the mouth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amylase"},{"label":"B","text":"Pepsin"},{"label":"C","text":"Lipase"},{"label":"D","text":"Trypsin"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Salivary amylase (ptyalin) breaks down starch into maltose in the oral cavity."},{"id":1474,"text":"Which of these is a vestigial organ in humans?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liver"},{"label":"B","text":"Heart"},{"label":"C","text":"Appendix"},{"label":"D","text":"Kidney"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Appendix is a vestigial organ (remnant of larger cecum in herbivore ancestors)."},{"id":1480,"text":"During DNA replication, which enzyme is responsible for synthesizing the new DNA strand by adding nucleotides complementary to the template strand?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DNA ligase"},{"label":"B","text":"DNA helicase"},{"label":"C","text":"DNA polymerase"},{"label":"D","text":"Primase"}],"answer":2,"solution":"During DNA replication, several enzymes play crucial roles:*   **DNA ligase:** Joins the Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand by forming phosphodiester bonds.*   **DNA helicase:** Unwinds the DNA double helix by breaking the hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs, creating the replication fork.*   **DNA polymerase:** This is the primary enzyme responsible for synthesizing new DNA strands. It adds deoxyribonucleotides one by one to the growing DNA strand, using the existing template strand as a guide to ensure complementary base pairing (A with T, G with C).*   **Primase:** Synthesizes short RNA primers, which are necessary for DNA polymerase to initiate DNA synthesis, as DNA polymerase cannot start a new strand from scratch."},{"id":1483,"text":"A person suffers from abnormally high blood glucose levels, frequent urination, and excessive thirst. Which of the following conditions is most likely causing these symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetes insipidus"},{"label":"B","text":"Hypothyroidism"},{"label":"C","text":"Diabetes mellitus"},{"label":"D","text":"Addison's disease"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The symptoms described are classic indicators of Diabetes Mellitus.*   **High blood glucose levels (hyperglycemia):** This is the hallmark of diabetes mellitus, caused by insufficient insulin production or insulin resistance, leading to glucose accumulation in the blood.*   **Frequent urination (polyuria):** High glucose levels in the blood lead to glucose excretion in the urine, drawing water with it due to osmosis, resulting in increased urine output.*   **Excessive thirst (polydipsia):** The loss of fluids through frequent urination leads to dehydration, triggering a sensation of intense thirst.Let's look at the other options:*   **Diabetes insipidus:** Characterized by frequent urination and thirst, but it is due to a deficiency of ADH (vasopressin) or kidney'sin ability to respond to ADH, and does not involve high blood glucose levels.*   **Hypothyroidism:** Caused by low thyroid hormone levels, leading to symptoms like weight gain, fatigue, cold intolerance, and slow metabolism, not primarily high blood glucose.*   **Addison's disease:** Results from adrenal insufficiency, affecting cortisol and aldosterone production, leading to symptoms like fatigue, weight loss, low blood pressure, and skin hyperpigmentation, not typically high blood glucose."},{"id":1489,"text":"Which of these is a prokaryote?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amoeba"},{"label":"B","text":"Bacteria"},{"label":"C","text":"Yeast"},{"label":"D","text":"Paramecium"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Bacteria are prokaryotes (no membrane-bound nucleus/organelles)."},{"id":1492,"text":"DNA replication is which type of process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conservative"},{"label":"B","text":"Semiconservative"},{"label":"C","text":"Dispersive"},{"label":"D","text":"Random"}],"answer":1,"solution":"DNA replication follows the semiconservative model as demonstrated by Meselson and Stahl."},{"id":1494,"text":"The pigment responsible for skin color is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlorophyll"},{"label":"B","text":"Haemoglobin"},{"label":"C","text":"Melanin"},{"label":"D","text":"Carotene"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Melanin produced by melanocytes determines skin, hair, and eye color."},{"id":1503,"text":"Which enzyme breaks down starch in the mouth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amylase"},{"label":"B","text":"Pepsin"},{"label":"C","text":"Lipase"},{"label":"D","text":"Trypsin"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Salivary amylase (ptyalin) breaks down starch into maltose in the oral cavity."},{"id":1506,"text":"The process of cell division for growth is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meiosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Mitosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Binary fission"},{"label":"D","text":"Budding"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Mitosis produces identical daughter cells for growth and repair."},{"id":1515,"text":"In a DNA double helix, the two strands are joined by hydrogen bonds between the nitrogenous bases. Which of the following statements about these bonds is correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Adenine forms two hydrogen bonds with Guanine."},{"label":"B","text":"Thymine forms three hydrogen bonds with Cytosine."},{"label":"C","text":"Adenine forms two hydrogen bonds with Thymine."},{"label":"D","text":"Guanine forms two hydrogen bonds with Cytosine."}],"answer":2,"solution":"In the DNA double helix, specific base pairing rules are followed, known as Chargaff's rules:\r\n- Adenine (A) always pairs with Thymine (T) via two hydrogen bonds.\r\n- Guanine (G) always pairs with Cytosine (C) via three hydrogen bonds.\r\n\r\nLet's evaluate the options:\r\nA) Adenine forms two hydrogen bonds with Guanine. (Incorrect, A pairs with T, Gpa irs with C)\r\nB) Thymine forms three hydrogen bonds with Cytosine. (Incorrect, T pairs with A via two H-bonds, C pairs with G via three H-bonds)\r\nC) Adenine forms two hydrogen bonds with Thymine. (Correct)\r\nD) Guanine forms two hydrogen bonds with Cytosine. (Incorrect, Gpa irs with C via three H-bonds)"},{"id":1517,"text":"Which enzyme breaks down starch in the mouth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amylase"},{"label":"B","text":"Pepsin"},{"label":"C","text":"Lipase"},{"label":"D","text":"Trypsin"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Salivary amylase (ptyalin) breaks down starch into maltose in the oral cavity."},{"id":1518,"text":"Which hormone regulates blood sugar level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroxine"},{"label":"B","text":"Insulin"},{"label":"C","text":"Adrenaline"},{"label":"D","text":"Estrogen"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Insulin lowers blood glucose, while glucagon raises it."},{"id":1524,"text":"Which organelle is involved in protein synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitochondria"},{"label":"B","text":"Ribosome"},{"label":"C","text":"Lysosome"},{"label":"D","text":"Golgi body"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Ribosomes (composed of rRNA + proteins) synthesize proteins."},{"id":1529,"text":"The number of chromosomes in human cells is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"23"},{"label":"B","text":"46"},{"label":"C","text":"48"},{"label":"D","text":"44"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Humans have 23 pairs = 46 chromosomes (22 autosomes + 1 sex chromosome pair)."},{"id":1532,"text":"Which disease is caused by a virus?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cholera"},{"label":"B","text":"Typhoid"},{"label":"C","text":"AIDS"},{"label":"D","text":"Malaria"}],"answer":2,"solution":"AIDS is caused by HIV (virus). Cholera/typhoid: bacteria, Malaria: protozoa."},{"id":1533,"text":"The enzyme that breaks down proteins in stomach is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amylase"},{"label":"B","text":"Pepsin"},{"label":"C","text":"Lipase"},{"label":"D","text":"Trypsin"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Pepsin (in gastric juice) breaks proteins into peptides in acidic medium."},{"id":1539,"text":"DNA replication is which type of process?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conservative"},{"label":"B","text":"Semiconservative"},{"label":"C","text":"Dispersive"},{"label":"D","text":"Random"}],"answer":1,"solution":"DNA replication follows the semiconservative model as demonstrated by Meselson and Stahl."},{"id":1553,"text":"Which organ is responsible for blood filtration?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Liver"},{"label":"B","text":"Kidney"},{"label":"C","text":"Heart"},{"label":"D","text":"Lungs"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Kidneys filter blood through nephrons, removing waste and excess water to form urine."},{"id":1554,"text":"The pigment responsible for skin color is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlorophyll"},{"label":"B","text":"Haemoglobin"},{"label":"C","text":"Melanin"},{"label":"D","text":"Carotene"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Melanin produced by melanocytes determines skin, hair, and eye color."},{"id":1561,"text":"The process of cell division for growth is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Meiosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Mitosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Binary fission"},{"label":"D","text":"Budding"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Mitosis produces identical daughter cells for growth and repair."},{"id":1562,"text":"The enzyme that breaks down proteins in stomach is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amylase"},{"label":"B","text":"Pepsin"},{"label":"C","text":"Lipase"},{"label":"D","text":"Trypsin"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Pepsin (in gastric juice) breaks proteins into peptides in acidic medium."},{"id":1564,"text":"What is the primary function of the mitochondria in a cell?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Protein synthesis"},{"label":"B","text":"Cell division"},{"label":"C","text":"Energy production"},{"label":"D","text":"Waste removal"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The primary function of the mitochondria is to generate energy for the cell through the process of cellular respiration."},{"id":1566,"text":"The green pigment in plants that traps sunlight is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Xanthophyll"},{"label":"B","text":"Chlorophyll"},{"label":"C","text":"Carotene"},{"label":"D","text":"Phycobilin"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Chlorophyll absorbs sunlight (mainly blue and red) for photosynthesis."},{"id":1567,"text":"The largest gland in the human body is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pancreas"},{"label":"B","text":"Liver"},{"label":"C","text":"Salivary gland"},{"label":"D","text":"Thyroid"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Liver is the largest gland (~1.5 kg) and performs 500+ functions."},{"id":1568,"text":"Which enzyme breaks down starch in the mouth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amylase"},{"label":"B","text":"Pepsin"},{"label":"C","text":"Lipase"},{"label":"D","text":"Trypsin"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Salivary amylase (ptyalin) breaks down starch into maltose in the oral cavity."},{"id":1571,"text":"Which vitamin is water soluble?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin A"},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin D"},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin C"},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin K"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Vitamin Cand B-complex are water-soluble. A, D, E, K aref at-soluble."},{"id":1572,"text":"The disease caused by deficiency of insulin is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Goiter"},{"label":"B","text":"Diabetes mellitus"},{"label":"C","text":"Anemia"},{"label":"D","text":"Rickets"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Diabetes mellitus is caused by insufficient insulin production or insulin resistance."},{"id":1575,"text":"What is the process by which water moves through a plant, from the roots to the leaves, and is then released into the air as water vapor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respiration"},{"label":"B","text":"Photosynthesis"},{"label":"C","text":"Transpiration"},{"label":"D","text":"Evaporation"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Transpiration is the process by which water moves through a plant, from the roots to the leaves, and is then released into the air as water vapor."},{"id":1578,"text":"Which hormone is called the \"fight or flight\" hormone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insulin"},{"label":"B","text":"Adrenaline"},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroxine"},{"label":"D","text":"Estrogen"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Adrenaline (epinephrine) prepares body for stress (increases HR, BP, etc.)."},{"id":1581,"text":"Blood group with no antigens on RBC is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"AB"},{"label":"D","text":"O"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Blood group O has no A or B antigens on RBCs but has both anti-A and anti-B antibodies."},{"id":1582,"text":"A person suffers from abnormally high blood glucose levels, frequent urination, and excessive thirst. Which of the following conditions is most likely causing these symptoms?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Diabetes insipidus"},{"label":"B","text":"Hypothyroidism"},{"label":"C","text":"Diabetes mellitus"},{"label":"D","text":"Addison's disease"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The symptoms described are classic indicators of Diabetes Mellitus.\r\n\r\n*   **High blood glucose levels (hyperglycemia):** This is the hallmark of diabetes mellitus, caused by insufficient insulin production or insulin resistance, leading to glucose accumulation in the blood.\r\n*   **Frequent urination (polyuria):** High glucose levels in the blood lead to glucose excretion in the urine, drawing water with it due to osmosis, resulting in increased urine output.\r\n*   **Excessive thirst (polydipsia):** The loss of fluids through frequent urination leads to dehydration, triggering a sensation of intense thirst.\r\n\r\nLet's look at the other options:\r\n*   **Diabetes insipidus:** Characterized by frequent urination and thirst, but it is due to a deficiency of ADH (vasopressin) or kidney'sin ability to respond to ADH, and does not involve high blood glucose levels.\r\n*   **Hypothyroidism:** Caused by low thyroid hormone levels, leading to symptoms like weight gain, fatigue, cold intolerance, and slow metabolism, not primarily high blood glucose.\r\n*   **Addison's disease:** Results from adrenal insufficiency, affecting cortisol and aldosterone production, leading to symptoms like fatigue, weight loss, low blood pressure, and skin hyperpigmentation, not typically high blood glucose."},{"id":1583,"text":"Which organelle is involved in protein synthesis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mitochondria"},{"label":"B","text":"Ribosome"},{"label":"C","text":"Lysosome"},{"label":"D","text":"Golgi body"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Ribosomes (composed of rRNA + proteins) synthesize proteins."},{"id":1588,"text":"The respiratory pigment in human blood is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlorophyll"},{"label":"B","text":"Hemoglobin"},{"label":"C","text":"Melanin"},{"label":"D","text":"Myoglobin"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Hemoglobin in RBCs transports Of rom lungs to tissues."},{"id":1589,"text":"Which part of the brain controls balance and coordination?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cerebrum"},{"label":"B","text":"Cerebellum"},{"label":"C","text":"Medulla"},{"label":"D","text":"Hypothalamus"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Cerebellum coordinates voluntary movements, balance, and posture."},{"id":1590,"text":"Which of the following hormones is secreted by the anterior pituitary gland?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)"},{"label":"B","text":"Oxytocin"},{"label":"C","text":"Growth hormone (GH)"},{"label":"D","text":"Melatonin"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The anterior pituitary gland secretes several hormones, including Growth Hormone (GH), Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH), Adrenocorticotropic Hormone (ACTH), Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH), Luteinizing Hormone (LH), and Prolactin. ADH and Oxytocin are secreted by the posterior pituitary, and Melatonin is secreted by the pineal gland."},{"id":1591,"text":"Which part of the nephron is impermeable to water but permeable to salts, leading to the dilution of filtrate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT)"},{"label":"B","text":"Descending limb of Henle's loop"},{"label":"C","text":"Ascending limb of Henle's loop"},{"label":"D","text":"Distal Convoluted Tubule (DCT)"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The ascending limb of Henle's loop is characterized by its impermeability to water. However, it actively reabsorbs sodium and chloride ions (and some other electrolytes) from the filtrate into the interstitial fluid. As solutes are removed from the filtrate but water is retained, the filtrate becomes progressively more dilute as it moves up the ascending limb. \r\n- PCT: Permeable to both water and salts, involved in bulk reabsorption.\r\n- Descending limb of Henle's loop: Highly permeable to water but largely impermeable to electrolytes, leading to concentration of filtrate.\r\n- DCT: Involved in conditional reabsorption of water and sodium, not primarily responsible for initial dilution due to impermeability to water while being permeable to salts."},{"id":1592,"text":"Which vitamin is produced in the skin on exposure to sunlight?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vitamin A"},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin D"},{"label":"C","text":"Vitamin C"},{"label":"D","text":"Vitamin B12"}],"answer":1,"solution":"UV rays convert 7-dehydrocholesterol to Vitamin D in the skin."},{"id":1602,"text":"During DNA replication, which enzyme is responsible for synthesizing the new DNA strand by adding nucleotides complementary to the template strand?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DNA ligase"},{"label":"B","text":"DNA helicase"},{"label":"C","text":"DNA polymerase"},{"label":"D","text":"Primase"}],"answer":2,"solution":"During DNA replication, several enzymes play crucial roles:\r\n\r\n*   **DNA ligase:** Joins the Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand by forming phosphodiester bonds.\r\n*   **DNA helicase:** Unwinds the DNA double helix by breaking the hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs, creating the replication fork.\r\n*   **DNA polymerase:** This is the primary enzyme responsible for synthesizing new DNA strands. It adds deoxyribonucleotides one by one to the growing DNA strand, using the existing template strand as a guide to ensure complementary base pairing (A with T, G with C).\r\n*   **Primase:** Synthesizes short RNA primers, which are necessary for DNA polymerase to initiate DNA synthesis, as DNA polymerase cannot start a new strand from scratch."},{"id":1616,"text":"What is the term for the process by which plants convert light energy into chemical energy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respiration"},{"label":"B","text":"Photosynthesis"},{"label":"C","text":"Fermentation"},{"label":"D","text":"Transpiration"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Photosynthesis is the process by which plants, algae, and some bacteria convert light energy from the sun into chemical energy in the form of glucose. This process occurs in specialized organelles called chloroplasts and is essential for life on Earth."},{"id":1619,"text":"The number of chromosomes in human cells is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"23"},{"label":"B","text":"46"},{"label":"C","text":"48"},{"label":"D","text":"44"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Humans have 23 pairs = 46 chromosomes (22 autosomes + 1 sex chromosome pair)."},{"id":1622,"text":"Which hormone is called the \"fight or flight\" hormone?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insulin"},{"label":"B","text":"Adrenaline"},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroxine"},{"label":"D","text":"Estrogen"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Adrenaline (epinephrine) prepares body for stress (increases HR, BP, etc.)."},{"id":1625,"text":"The number of chromosomes in human cells is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"23"},{"label":"B","text":"46"},{"label":"C","text":"48"},{"label":"D","text":"44"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Humans have 23 pairs = 46 chromosomes (22 autosomes + 1 sex chromosome pair)."},{"id":1629,"text":"The universal donor blood group is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"AB"},{"label":"D","text":"O"}],"answer":3,"solution":"O negative is universal donor (no A/B antigens, no Rh factor)."},{"id":1633,"text":"The process of DNA replication is described ass emi-conservative. What does this imply?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Each new DNA molecule consists of two entirely new strands."},{"label":"B","text":"Each new DNA molecule consists of one original strand and one newly synthesized strand."},{"label":"C","text":"The DNA molecule breaks down completely and reforms into new strands."},{"label":"D","text":"The replication process is only about 50% efficient."}],"answer":1,"solution":"Semi-conservative replication means that each daughter DNA molecule contains one strand from the parent molecule and one newly synthesized strand."},{"id":1635,"text":"The respiratory pigment in human blood is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chlorophyll"},{"label":"B","text":"Hemoglobin"},{"label":"C","text":"Melanin"},{"label":"D","text":"Myoglobin"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Hemoglobin in RBCs transports Of rom lungs to tissues."},{"id":1636,"text":"Which hormone regulates blood sugar level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thyroxine"},{"label":"B","text":"Insulin"},{"label":"C","text":"Adrenaline"},{"label":"D","text":"Estrogen"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Insulin lowers blood glucose, while glucagon raises it."},{"id":1638,"text":"What is the process by which water moves through a plant, from the roots to the leaves, and is then released into the air as water vapor?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respiration"},{"label":"B","text":"Photosynthesis"},{"label":"C","text":"Transpiration"},{"label":"D","text":"Evaporation"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Transpiration is the process by which water moves through a plant, from the roots to the leaves, and is then released into the air as water vapor."},{"id":1640,"text":"What is the term for the process by which plants convert light energy into chemical energy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respiration"},{"label":"B","text":"Photosynthesis"},{"label":"C","text":"Decomposition"},{"label":"D","text":"Fermentation"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Photosynthesis is the process by which plants convert light energy into chemical energy."},{"id":1642,"text":"What is the term for the process by which water moves through a plant, from the roots to the leaves?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Respiration"},{"label":"B","text":"Photosynthesis"},{"label":"C","text":"Transpiration"},{"label":"D","text":"Evaporation"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Transpiration is the process by which water moves through a plant, from the roots to the leaves, and is then released into the air as water vapor. This process is an important part of the water cycle and helps to regulate the plant's temperature and maintain its water balance."},{"id":1644,"text":"What is the term for the process by which an organism's genetic information is used to synthesize proteins and other molecules in response to environmental stimuli?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hereditary"},{"label":"B","text":"Genetic drift"},{"label":"C","text":"Gene expression"},{"label":"D","text":"Cell division"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Gene expression is the process by which an organism's genetic information is used to synthesize proteins and other molecules in response to environmental stimuli. This involves the transcription of DNA into RNA and the translation of RNA into protein, and is essential for the proper functioning of the organism."}]},{"name":"Zoology","count":40,"questions":[{"id":1652,"text":"Which of the following structures in a neuron is primarily responsible for receiving signals from other neurons or sensory receptors?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Axon"},{"label":"B","text":"Dendrite"},{"label":"C","text":"Myelin sheath"},{"label":"D","text":"Node of Ranvier"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Dendrites are branched extensions of the neuron specifically designed to receive incoming electrical and chemical signals. The axon conducts impulses away from the cell body, the myelin sheath insulates the axon for faster transmission, and the node of Ranvier is a gap in the myelin where saltatory conduction occurs."},{"id":1653,"text":"In humans, the primary function of the thymus gland during childhood is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Production of insulin"},{"label":"B","text":"Maturation of T-lymphocytes"},{"label":"C","text":"Secretion of adrenaline"},{"label":"D","text":"Regulation of blood calcium levels"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The thymus gland is crucial for the development and maturation of T-lymphocytes (T-cells), which are essential for adaptive immunity. Insulin is produced by the pancreas, adrenaline by the adrenal medulla, and blood calcium regulation involves the parathyroid glands."},{"id":1654,"text":"Which of the following pairs represents a correct match between an animal phylum and a characteristic feature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Porifera - Presence of true tissues"},{"label":"B","text":"Cnidaria - Radial symmetry and cnidocytes"},{"label":"C","text":"Annelida - Segmented body with jointed appendages"},{"label":"D","text":"Arthropoda - Soft unsegmented body"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Cnidarians exhibit radial symmetry and possess specialized stinging cells called cnidocytes. Poriferans lack true tissues; Annelids have segments but lack jointed appendages (that is a trait of Arthropods); Arthropods have hard exoskeletons and jointed legs, not soft unsegmented bodies."},{"id":1655,"text":"The evolutionary trend towards the development of wings in birds and bats is an example of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Homology"},{"label":"B","text":"Analogy (Convergent Evolution)"},{"label":"C","text":"Vestigiality"},{"label":"D","text":"Genetic Drift"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Wings in birds and bats are analogous structures because they evolved independently for flight in different lineages, making this convergent evolution. Homology refers to similar structures due to common ancestry; vestigiality refers to reduced organs with no function."},{"id":1656,"text":"Which of the following blood groups is considered the universal donor for red blood cell transfusions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A+"},{"label":"B","text":"B-"},{"label":"C","text":"AB+"},{"label":"D","text":"O-"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Blood group O-negative lacks A, B, and Rh antigens on the red blood cells, making it safe to transfuse into recipients of any blood type. A+ has A antigen and Rh factor; B- has B antigen; AB+ has both A and B antigens, limiting their compatibility."},{"id":1657,"text":"The process by which water evaporates from the leaves of a plant, creating a suction force that pulls water up from the roots, is called:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Translocation"},{"label":"B","text":"Photosynthesis"},{"label":"C","text":"Transpiration"},{"label":"D","text":"Osmosis"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Transpiration is the evaporation of water from plant surfaces (mainly stomata) which generates negative pressure to pull water upward. Translocation refers to sugar transport; photosynthesis converts light to chemical energy; osmosis is passive water movement across a membrane."},{"id":1658,"text":"Which of the following hormones acts as a counter-regulatory hormone to insulin by raising blood glucose levels?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insulin"},{"label":"B","text":"Glucagon"},{"label":"C","text":"Thyroxine"},{"label":"D","text":"Melatonin"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Glucagon is secreted by the pancreas when blood sugar is low and stimulates glycogen breakdown to raise glucose levels. Insulin lowers blood sugar; Thyroxine regulates metabolism but does not directly oppose insulin'sim mediate effect on glucose; Melatonin regulates sleep cycles."},{"id":1659,"text":"In the human circulatory system, which component of the blood is primarily responsible for clotting?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Red Blood Cells"},{"label":"B","text":"White Blood Cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Platelets"},{"label":"D","text":"Plasma"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Platelets (thrombocytes) are cell fragments that aggregate at injury sites to form a plug and release factors initiating the clotting cascade. Red blood cells transport oxygen; white blood cells fight infection; plasma is the liquid medium carrying dissolved substances."},{"id":1660,"text":"Which of the following organisms exhibits metamorphosis during its life cycle?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Frog"},{"label":"B","text":"Earthworm"},{"label":"C","text":"Penguin"},{"label":"D","text":"Jellyfish"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Frogs undergo distinct metamorphosis from an aquatic tadpole stage to a terrestrial adult frog. Earthworms have direct development; penguins are mammals with no larval stage; while some jellyfish have complex life cycles involving polyp and medusa stages, 'metamorphosis' in the standard zoological context of vertebrate exams most distinctly applies here."},{"id":1661,"text":"The presence of a diaphragm is a defining characteristic of which group of animals?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reptiles"},{"label":"B","text":"Birds"},{"label":"C","text":"Mammals"},{"label":"D","text":"Amphibians"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The diaphragm is a muscular partition separating the thoracic and abdominal cavities, essential for efficient breathing in mammals. Reptiles, birds, and amphibians rely on different mechanisms (rib movement or buccal pumping) and lack this specific structure."},{"id":1662,"text":"Which of the following structures is NOT part of the human male reproductive system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Seminal Vesicle"},{"label":"B","text":"Ovary"},{"label":"C","text":"Prostate Gland"},{"label":"D","text":"Epididymis"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The ovary is a female reproductive organ responsible for egg production. The seminal vesicle and prostate gland contribute to semen production in males, and the epididymis stores and matures sperm."},{"id":1663,"text":"What is the primary function of the stomata found on the epidermis of plant leaves?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gas exchange and transpiration"},{"label":"B","text":"Photosynthesis only"},{"label":"C","text":"Water absorption from soil"},{"label":"D","text":"Defense against herbivores"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Stomata are microscopic pores that open to allow carbon dioxide intake for photosynthesis and release oxygen and water vapor (transpiration). Photosynthesis occurs in chloroplasts within cells, not the pore itself; water absorption is done by roots; defense involves trichomes or chemical compounds."},{"id":1664,"text":"Which of the following structures in the human heart is responsible for preventing the backflow of blood from the left ventricle to the left atrium during systole?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tricuspid valve"},{"label":"B","text":"Mitral (bicuspid) valve"},{"label":"C","text":"Pulmonary semilunar valve"},{"label":"D","text":"Aortic semilunar valve"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The Mitral (bicuspid) valve is located between the left atrium and left ventricle, ensuring unidirectional flow. The Tricuspid valve is on the right side, while both semilunar valves prevent backflow into the arteries during ejection."},{"id":1665,"text":"In the context of plant physiology, which hormone is primarily responsible for promoting cell elongation and stem growth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abscisic acid"},{"label":"B","text":"Gibberellins"},{"label":"C","text":"Cytokinins"},{"label":"D","text":"Ethylene"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Gibberellins stimulate cell division and elongation, leading to stem growth. Abscisic acid inhibits growth and promotes dormancy, Cytokinins promote cell division but not primarily elongation, and Ethylene regulates fruit ripening."},{"id":1666,"text":"Which of the following characteristics is unique to mammals among vertebrates?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of a three-chambered heart"},{"label":"B","text":"Feathers for flight"},{"label":"C","text":"Production of milk by mammary glands"},{"label":"D","text":"Cold-blooded metabolism"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Mammals are defined by the presence of mammary glands that produce milk. Amphibians and reptiles have different heart structures; birds have feathers but lay eggs; reptiles and amphibians are generally cold-blooded."},{"id":1667,"text":"The process by which ammonia produced from amino acid deamination is converted into urea in the liver is an example of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glycolysis"},{"label":"B","text":"Urea Cycle"},{"label":"C","text":"Krebs Cycle"},{"label":"D","text":"Electron Transport Chain"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The Urea Cycle (Ornithine Cycle) specifically converts toxic ammonia into less toxic urea for excretion. Glycolysis breaks down glucose, the Krebs Cycle generates ATP from acetyl-CoA, and the Electron Transport Chain produces ATP via oxidative phosphorylation."},{"id":1668,"text":"Which of the following blood cells plays the most critical role in the immune response by engulfing pathogens?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythrocytes"},{"label":"B","text":"Thrombocytes"},{"label":"C","text":"Neutrophils"},{"label":"D","text":"Plasma proteins"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Neutrophils are phagocytic white blood cells that engulf and destroy bacteria. Erythrocytes carry oxygen, Thrombocytes are involved in clotting, and Plasma proteins transport substances but do not directly engulf pathogens."},{"id":1669,"text":"In birds, the presence of a four-chambered heart is an adaptation that supports which physiological requirement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cold-blooded metabolism"},{"label":"B","text":"Low oxygen demand"},{"label":"C","text":"High metabolic rate and endothermy"},{"label":"D","text":"Aquatic respiration"}],"answer":2,"solution":"A four-chambered heart allows complete separation of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood, supporting the high metabolic rate needed for flight and maintaining constant body temperature (endothermy). Cold-blooded animals typically have three-chambered hearts."},{"id":1670,"text":"Which of the following structures in the human ear is directly responsible for converting sound vibrations into electrical signals sent to the brain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tympanic membrane"},{"label":"B","text":"Ossicles"},{"label":"C","text":"Organ of Corti"},{"label":"D","text":"Semicircular canals"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The Organ of Corti contains hair cells that transduce mechanical vibrations into nerve impulses. The Tympanic membrane receives sound waves, the Ossicles amplify them, and the Semicircular canals detect balance."},{"id":1671,"text":"Which of the following organisms exhibits a life cycle with distinct larval and adult stages involving metamorphosis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Earthworm"},{"label":"B","text":"Frog"},{"label":"C","text":"Snake"},{"label":"D","text":"Shark"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Frogs undergo complete metamorphosis from tadpole to adult frog. Earthworms, snakes (reptiles), and sharks generally do not exhibit such distinct metamorphic stages in their life cycles."},{"id":1684,"text":"Which of the following statements correctly describes the role of the vasa recta in the counter-current mechanism of the mammalian kidney?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It actively transports NaCl into the medullary interstitium to maintain high osmolarity."},{"label":"B","text":"It removes excess water and solutes from the medulla without disrupting the corticomedullary osmotic gradient."},{"label":"C","text":"It facilitates the passive diffusion of urea from the collecting duct into the medullary interstitium."},{"label":"D","text":"It serves as the primary site for the reabsorption of glucose and amino acids via secondary active transport."}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut/Trick: Use option elimination based on anatomical location and function. Option A describes the thick ascending limb of Henle's loop (active transport). Option D describes the Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT). Option Cde scribes urea transporters in the collecting duct. The vasa recta are straight capillaries acting as counter-current exchangers; their sole role is to supply blood/nutrients and remove reabsorbed water/solutes *without* washing away the gradient (passive equilibration). Step-by-step: 1. Identify the vasa recta as peritubular capillaries running parallel to Henle's loop. 2. Recall the Counter-Current Multiplier (Loop of Henle) creates the gradient; the Counter-Current Exchanger (Vasa Recta) preserves it. 3. Blood flows down descending limb (loses water, gains solute) and up ascending limb (gains water, loses solute). 4. Net effect: U-shaped flow prevents dissipation of medullary hyperosmolarity while allowing waste removal. Option B matches this definition perfectly."},{"id":1685,"text":"The water vascular system, a unique hydraulic network utilized for locomotion, feeding, and respiration, is a defining characteristic exclusive to which phylum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mollusca"},{"label":"B","text":"Arthropoda"},{"label":"C","text":"Echinodermata"},{"label":"D","text":"Annelida"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut/Trick: Keyword association / Diagnostic Feature Mapping. 'Water Vascular System' + 'Tube Feet' + 'Pentaradial Symmetry' = Echinodermata (Starfish, Sea Urchin). Eliminate others instantly: Mollusca (Radula, Mantle, Foot); Arthropoda (Chitinous Exoskeleton, Jointed Appendages, Haemocoel); Annelida (Metameric Segmentation, Setae, Closed Circulation). Step-by-step: 1. Recall the defining synapomorphies for major non-chordate phyla. 2. Phylum Echinodermata is uniquely defined by the water vascular system (derived from coelom) which operates hydraulic tube feet (podia). 3. Verify distractors: Mollusca uses a muscular foot; Arthropoda uses jointed legs; Annelida uses hydrostatic skeleton with setae/parapodia. 4. Select Option C (Echinodermata) as the only phylum possessing this system."},{"id":1686,"text":"At the neuromuscular junction, the binding of acetylcholine (ACh) to its specific receptors on the motor end plate directly results in which of the following immediate events?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Opening of ligand-gated sodium/potassium channels causing depolarization (End Plate Potential)."},{"label":"B","text":"Opening of voltage-gated calcium channels in the sarcolemma."},{"label":"C","text":"Release of calcium ions from the sarcoplasmic reticulum."},{"label":"D","text":"Activation of acetylcholinesterase to hydrolyze the neurotransmitter."}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut/Trick: Receptor Classification Logic. ACh binds to Nicotinic Acetylcholine Receptors (nAChR) on the motor end plate. These are *Ligand-Gated Ion Channels* (Channel-linked receptors). Binding = Conformational change = Channel Opening = Na+ influx / K+ efflux = Depolarization (EPP). Eliminate B (Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels are on the *pre-synaptic* terminal for ACh release). Eliminate C (SR Ca2+ release occurs later via T-tubules/DHPR-RyR coupling - Excitation-Contraction Coupling). Eliminate D (Acetylcholinesterase terminates signal *after* depolarization, it is not the *direct result* of binding). Step-by-step: 1. Action potential arrives at pre-synaptic terminal -> Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels open -> Ca2+ influx -> ACh exocytosis. 2. ACh diffuses across synaptic cleft. 3. ACh binds ton achr (Ligand-gated Na+/K+ channels) on post-synaptic membrane (Motor End Plate). 4. Channels open -> Na+ influx > K+ efflux -> Local Depolarization (End Plate Potential, EPP). 5. EPP spreads -> Voltage-gated Na+ channels open -> Muscle Action Potential -> T-tubules -> SR Ca2+ release. Option A describes Step 3-4 directly."},{"id":1695,"text":"Pulmonary surfactant, secreted by type II alveolar cells, primarily functions to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease surface tension in the alveoli"},{"label":"B","text":"Increase surface tension in the alveoli"},{"label":"C","text":"Directly enhance diffusion of gases across the respiratory membrane"},{"label":"D","text":"Produce mucus for trapping inhaled particles"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Surfactant is a 'surface‑active agent' – its name indicates it reduces surface tension. Step‑by‑step: Type II pneumocytes secrete surfactant, a lipoprotein complex that lowers alveolar surface tension, preventing collapse (atelectasis) during expiration and reducing the work of breathing."},{"id":1696,"text":"Which of the following is NOT a defining characteristic of phylum Chordata?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Presence of a notochord at some developmental stage"},{"label":"B","text":"A ventral, solid nerve cord"},{"label":"C","text":"Pharyngeal gill slits or pouches"},{"label":"D","text":"A post‑anal tail"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Chordates have a dorsal, hollow nerve cord; a ventral solid cord is typical of non‑chordates like annelids. Step‑by‑step: The four key chordate features are notochord, dorsal hollow nerve cord, pharyngeal slits, and post‑anal tail. Option B describes the opposite nerve cord arrangement, so it is not a chordate trait."},{"id":1697,"text":"The hormone secreted by the delta (δ) cells of the pancreatic islets of Langerhans is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Insulin"},{"label":"B","text":"Glucagon"},{"label":"C","text":"Somatostatin"},{"label":"D","text":"Pancreatic polypeptide"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Remember the Greek letters: α‑cells → glucagon, β‑cells → insulin, δ‑cells → somatostatin. Step‑by‑step: Pancreatic islets contain alpha, beta, delta, and PP cells. Delta cells produce somatostatin, which inhibits both insulin and glucagon release, regulating nutrient absorption."},{"id":1714,"text":"Enterokinase (enteropeptidase), secreted by the duodenal mucosa, acts as the master activator for pancreatic proteolytic zymogens. What is its specific and direct substrate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chymotrypsinogen"},{"label":"B","text":"Trypsinogen"},{"label":"C","text":"Procarboxypeptidase"},{"label":"D","text":"Pepsinogen"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Enterokinase uniquely initiates the pancreatic cascade by cleaving trypsinogen; trypsin then activates all other zymogens (chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase). Eliminate options activated by trypsin (A, C) and the gastric enzyme precursor (D). Full Solution: Enterokinase specifically hydrolyzes then -terminal activation peptide (Val-Asp-Asp-Asp-Asp-Lys) from trypsinogen to form active trypsin. Trypsin subsequently activates chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidases, and proelastase. Pepsinogen is activated by HCl and pepsin in the stomach."},{"id":1715,"text":"Identify the true coelomate (eucoelomate) among the following organisms, characterized by a body cavity completely lined by mesoderm-derived peritoneum.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ascaris lumbricoides"},{"label":"B","text":"Fasciola hepatica"},{"label":"C","text":"Pheretima posthuma"},{"label":"D","text":"Taenia solium"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Classify by phylum coelom type: Platyhelminthes (Fasciola, Taenia) = Acoelomates; Nematoda (Ascaris) = Pseudocoelomates; Annelida (Pheretima) = Eucoelomates. Eliminate A, B, D based on phylum. Full Solution: True coelom (schizocoel) is a defining feature of Annelida. Pheretima posthuma (earthworm) possesses a coelom lined by parietal and visceral peritoneum. Ascaris (Nematoda) has a pseudocoel (blastocoel persistence). Fasciola and Taenia (Platyhelminthes) are acoelomates with solid parenchyma."},{"id":1716,"text":"The Na+/K+-ATPase pump maintains the resting membrane potential in neurons by actively transporting ions against their electrochemical gradients. What is the stoichiometric ratio of ions transported per ATP hydrolyzed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3 Na+ out : 2 K+ in"},{"label":"B","text":"2 Na+ out : 3 K+ in"},{"label":"C","text":"3 Na+ in : 2 K+ out"},{"label":"D","text":"2 Na+ in : 3 K+ out"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Mnemonic '3-2-Out-In' for Na-K pump. It exports 3 Na+ and imports 2 K+, making it electrogenic (net +1 charge out). Eliminate C, D (wrong direction against gradients). Eliminate B (reversed ratio would be electroneutral/less negative). Full Solution: The pump binds 3 intracellular Na+ and 2 extracellular K+ per cycle. Phosphorylation drives conformational change releasing Na+ outside and K+ inside. This 3:2 efflux/influx creates a net outward current, directly contributing ~-5 to -10 mV to the resting potential."},{"id":1729,"text":"In a standard Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) cycle, the denaturation step requires the highest temperature to separate the double-stranded DNA template. What is the typical temperature range used for this denaturation step?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50–65 °C"},{"label":"B","text":"72–78 °C"},{"label":"C","text":"94–98 °C"},{"label":"D","text":"37–42 °C"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Use option elimination based on temperature hierarchy. Denaturation > Extension > Annealing. The highest range among options is 94–98 °C. Full Solution: PCR involves three main steps: Denaturation (94–98 °C) breaks hydrogen bonds between DNA strands; Annealing (50–65 °C) allows primers to bind; Extension (72–78 °C) is the optimal temperature for Taq polymerase activity. Option A is the annealing range, Option B is the extension range, and Option D is typical for ligation or reverse transcription."},{"id":1730,"text":"An immunoglobulin G (IgG) molecule is treated with excess β-mercaptoethanol (a reducing agent) and subjected to SDS-PAGE under reducing conditions. How many distinct polypeptide bands will be visualized on the gel?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"One"},{"label":"B","text":"Three"},{"label":"C","text":"Two"},{"label":"D","text":"Four"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: IgG basic structure is H₂L₂ (two identical Heavy chains ~50 kDa, two identical Light chains ~25 kDa) linked by disulfide bonds. Reducing agents break S-S bonds. SDS-PAGE separates by molecular weight. Only two unique chain sizes exist → Two bands. Full Solution: Native IgG is a tetramer (2 Heavy + 2 Light chains) held by inter-chain disulfide bonds. β-mercaptoethanol reduces these bonds, dissociating the molecule into its monomeric polypeptide subunits. Since the two heavy chains are identical and the two light chains are identical, SDS-PAGE resolves them into two distinct bands corresponding to the Heavy chain (~50 kDa) and Light chain (~25 kDa)."},{"id":1731,"text":"In a large, randomly mating population in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, the incidence of an autosomal recessive disorder is 1 in 2500 individuals. What is the approximate frequency of heterozygous carriers in this population?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1/25"},{"label":"B","text":"1/50"},{"label":"C","text":"1/100"},{"label":"D","text":"1/125"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: For rare recessive disorders (q² << 1), p ≈ 1. Carrier frequency (2pq) ≈ 2q = 2√(q²). Here q² = 1/2500, so q = 1/50. Approximate carrier frequency = 2 × (1/50) = 1/25. Step-by-step: Given q² = 1/2500. Calculate q = √(1/2500) = 1/50. Calculate p = 1 - q = 1 - 1/50 = 49/50. Carrier frequency (2pq) = 2 × (49/50) × (1/50) = 98/2500 = 49/1250 ≈ 0.0392. 1/25 = 0.04. The approximation 2q = 1/25 = 0.04 is very close and the standard exam shortcut."},{"id":1745,"text":"Which of the following organisms possesses a copper-containing respiratory pigment (Hemocyanin) dissolved in its plasma, giving its oxygenated blood a characteristic blue color?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Earthworm (Pheretima)"},{"label":"B","text":"Cockroach (Periplaneta)"},{"label":"C","text":"Snail (Pila)"},{"label":"D","text":"Frog (Rana)"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: **Phylum-based Pigment Rule**. **Mollusca** (Snail, Octopus) & **Crustacea** (Prawn, Crab) → **Hemocyanin (Cu, Blue)**. **Annelida** (Earthworm) & **Chordata** (Frog) → **Hemoglobin (Fe, Red)**. **Insecta** (Cockroach) → **No pigment** (Tracheal system delivers O2 directly). Full Solution: Hemocyanin is a copper-based respiratory pigment found in the phyla Mollusca and Arthropoda (specifically subphylum Crustacea). Snail (Pila) belongs to Mollusca and uses hemocyanin. Earthworm (Annelida) uses hemoglobin dissolved in plasma. Cockroach (Insecta) lacks respiratory pigments entirely, relying on a network of tracheae for gas exchange. Frog (Chordata/Amphibia) uses hemoglobin packed inside Red Blood Cells (RBCs)."},{"id":1746,"text":"In the *lac* operon of *E. coli*, the Catabolite Activator Protein (CAP) complexed with cAMP binds to a specific DNA site to enhance transcription. What is the primary mechanism of this enhancement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Binds to the CAP site upstream of the promoter, facilitating RNA polymerase binding"},{"label":"B","text":"Binds to the operator region, preventing repressor protein binding"},{"label":"C","text":"Acts as an allosteric inducer by binding directly to the repressor protein"},{"label":"D","text":"Is synthesized in high amounts when glucose concentration in the medium is high"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: **CAP-cAMP = **C**atabolite **A**ctivator **P**rotein**. **Low Glucose → High cAMP → CAP-cAMP binds CAP site (upstream) → Helps RNA Pol bind Promoter → Transcription ON**. High Glucose → Low cAMP → CAP inactive → Transcription LOW (Catabolite Repression). Full Solution: The *lac* operon is regulated by both negative control (Lac Repressor) and positive control (CAP-cAMP). When glucose is scarce, cAMP levels rise. cAMP binds to CAP (CRP). The CAP-cAMP complex binds to the **CAP binding site** located upstream of the promoter. This binding induces a bend in the DNA, which facilitates the binding of RNA Polymerase to the promoter, significantly increasing the transcription rate of structural genes (lac Z, Y, A). It does not bind the operator (Option B), nor does it bind the repressor (Option C - allolactose does). Option D is factually incorrect as cAMP levels are *low* when glucose is high."},{"id":1759,"text":"The Juxtaglomerular Apparatus (JGA) regulates renal blood flow and filtration rate. Which statement accurately describes the stimulus and secretory response of the juxtaglomerular (JG) cells?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased renal perfusion pressure stimulates JG cells to secrete renin."},{"label":"B","text":"Increased NaCl concentration at the macula densa stimulates JG cells to secrete renin."},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased sympathetic stimulation inhibits JG cells from secreting erythropoietin."},{"label":"D","text":"Increased atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) stimulates JG cells to secrete angiotensinogen."}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: JG cells secrete Renin (not EPO or Angiotensinogen). Renin release is triggered by Low BP (baroreceptor), Low NaCl (macula densa), and Sympathetic stimulation. Eliminate options with wrong hormone (C, D) or inverse stimulus (B: High NaCl inhibits renin; D: ANP inhibits renin). Step-by-step: JG cells are modified afferent arteriolar smooth muscle cells acting as baroreceptors. A drop in renal perfusion pressure directly stimulates renin secretion. The macula densa senses low tubular NaCl (not high) to stimulate renin. Erythropoietin is produced by peritubular fibroblasts, not JG cells. Angiotensinogen is a hepatic protein; ANP suppresses renin release."},{"id":1760,"text":"The water vascular system is a unique feature of phylum Echinodermata. In a starfish (Asterias), which of the following structures is **not** a component of this system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Madreporite"},{"label":"B","text":"Nephridium"},{"label":"C","text":"Stone canal"},{"label":"D","text":"Ring canal"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Water Vascular System (WVS) pathway keywords: Madreporite → Stone canal → Ring canal → Radial canals → Tube feet. 'Nephridium' is an excretory organ of Annelida/Mollusca; Echinoderms lack nephridia. Eliminate A, C, D as they are sequential WVS components. Step-by-step: The WVS in Asterias begins at the madreporite (sieve plate), connects via the stone canal to the ring canal around the mouth. Radial canals extend from the ring canal to the arms, ending in tube feet. Nephridia are tubular excretory structures absent in echinoderms; nitrogenous waste diffuses out via tube feet and papulae."},{"id":1761,"text":"As per the table below showing relative serum hormone levels on Day 1, Day 14, and Day 21 of a 28-day human menstrual cycle, which hormone corresponds to H3? | Hormone | Day 1 (Menstruation) | Day 14 (Ovulation) | Day 21 (Mid-Luteal) | | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | | H1 | Low | **Peak** | Low | | H2 | Low | **Peak** | Moderate | | H3 | Low | Low | **Peak** | | H4 | Moderate | Low | **Peak** |","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Follicle Stimulating Hormone (FSH)"},{"label":"B","text":"Luteinizing Hormone (LH)"},{"label":"C","text":"Estradiol (Estrogen)"},{"label":"D","text":"Progesterone"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Identify the 'Day 21 Exclusive Peak' (Luteal Phase marker). Progesterone is the dominant luteal hormone (Corpus Luteum). LH/FSH peak sharply at Day 14 only. Estradiol peaks at both Day 14 (follicular) and Day 21 (luteal). Step-by-step: Day 14 peaks (H1, H2) represent the LH surge (sharp) and FSH surge. H4 (Moderate Day 1, Low Day 14, Peak Day 21) fits FSH (early follicular rise) or Estradiol (biphasic). H3 shows Low values until a distinct Peak at Day 21, characteristic of Progesterone secreted by the Corpus Luteum during the luteal phase."},{"id":1774,"text":"In which subphylum of Phylum Chordata does the notochord persist throughout life and extend the entire length of the body, including the anterior region?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cephalochordata"},{"label":"B","text":"Urochordata"},{"label":"C","text":"Vertebrata"},{"label":"D","text":"Hemichordata"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: 'Cephalo' means head; in Cephalochordata (e.g., Branchiostoma), the notochord extends into the head region (rostrum) and persists lifelong. Urochordates have notochord only in the larval tail; Vertebrates replace it with a vertebral column; Hemichordates possess a stomochord, not a true notochord. Full Solution: The defining feature of Chordata is the notochord. In subphylum Cephalochordata, the notochord is persistent, extends the full body length (including anterior to the nerve cord), and is contractile. In Urochordata, it is restricted to the larval tail and lost during metamorphosis. In Vertebrata, the embryonic notochord is largely replaced by the vertebral column. Hemichordata aren on-chordates with a buccal diverticulum (stomochord)."},{"id":1775,"text":"During the chloride shift (Hamburger phenomenon) in systemic capillaries, the efflux of bicarbonate ions (HCO₃⁻) from erythrocytes into the plasma is electrically balanced by the influx of which ion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium (Na⁺)"},{"label":"B","text":"Potassium (K⁺)"},{"label":"C","text":"Chloride (Cl⁻)"},{"label":"D","text":"Hydrogen (H⁺)"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: The process is named 'Chloride Shift'; therefore, Chloride ions (Cl⁻) move into the RBC to maintain electrochemical neutrality as HCO₃⁻ moves out. Full Solution: In tissues, CO₂ diffuses into RBCs and forms H₂CO₃ via carbonic anhydrase, dissociating into H⁺ (buffered by Hb) and HCO₃⁻. HCO₃⁻ diffuses out down its concentration gradient. To maintain electrical neutrality, Cl⁻ moves from plasma into the RBC via the Band 3 anion exchanger (AE1). This 1:1 exchange of HCO₃⁻ and Cl⁻ is the Chloride Shift. In pulmonary capillaries, the reverse occurs."},{"id":1776,"text":"Which enzyme in *E. coli* possesses 5'→3' exonuclease activity specifically required for the removal of RNA primers during DNA replication?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"DNA Polymerase III"},{"label":"B","text":"DNA Polymerase I"},{"label":"C","text":"DNA Ligase"},{"label":"D","text":"Primase"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Pol I = 'I' for Incision/Removal of primers (5'→3' exonuclease activity for nick translation). Pol III = Main synthesis (high processivity, 3'→5' proofreading). Ligase = Joins fragments. Primase = Synthesizes primers. Full Solution: DNA Polymerase I removes RNA primers via its 5'→3' exonuclease activity while simultaneously synthesizing DNA in the 5'→3' direction (nick translation) to fill the gaps. DNA Polymerase III is the primary replicative polymerase with high processivity but lacks 5'→3' exonuclease activity. DNA Ligase then seals the remaining nicks by forming phosphodiester bonds between adjacent nucleotides."}]},{"name":"Botany","count":28,"questions":[{"id":1680,"text":"In a dihybrid cross between two heterozygous parents (AaBb × AaBb), the F₂ population consists of 1600 plants. How many plants are expected to be homozygous dominant for both traits (AABB)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"100"},{"label":"B","text":"200"},{"label":"C","text":"300"},{"label":"D","text":"400"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: The F₂ phenotypic ratio is 9:3:3:1 (sum = 16 parts). The genotype AABB constitutes 1 part out of 16. Number = 1600 ÷ 16 = 100. Worked: A 4×4 Punnett square yields 16 genotypic combinations; AABB appears only once. Thus, frequency = 1/16. 1/16 × 1600 = 100 plants."},{"id":1681,"text":"How many ATP molecules are generated by substrate-level phosphorylation in the Krebs cycle per molecule of glucose oxidised aerobically?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"6"},{"label":"D","text":"8"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: One glucose yields 2 Acetyl CoA. Each Krebs cycle turn produces 1 GTP (≡ ATP) at succinyl-CoA synthetase step. Total = 2 turns × 1 ATP = 2 ATP. Worked: Glycolysis gives 2 ATP (substrate-level), Krebs gives 2 GTP (substrate-level), and the bulk (~32-34 ATP) comes from oxidative phosphorylation via NADH/FADH₂."},{"id":1682,"text":"A plant cell has a solute potential (Ψs) of –10 bars and a pressure potential (Ψp) of +4 bars. What is the water potential (Ψw) of the cell?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"–14 bars"},{"label":"B","text":"–6 bars"},{"label":"C","text":"+6 bars"},{"label":"D","text":"+14 bars"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Water potential Ψw = Ψs + Ψp. Algebraic sum: (–10) + (+4) = –6 bars. Eliminate positive options (C, D) since Ψs is negative and larger in magnitude than Ψp. Worked: Ψs is always negative; Ψp is positive in turgid cells. Ψw = –10 + 4 = –6 bars. Water moves from higher (less negative) to lower (more negative) potential."},{"id":1683,"text":"If 250 microspore mother cells in an anther undergo meiosis successfully, how many viable pollen grains will be formed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"250"},{"label":"B","text":"500"},{"label":"C","text":"750"},{"label":"D","text":"1000"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: 1 Microspore Mother Cell (2n) → Meiosis → 4 haploid microspores (pollen grains). No degeneration in male gametogenesis. Total = 250 × 4 = 1000. Worked: Microsporogenesis involves one meiotic division (Meiosis I & II) producing a tetrad of 4 microspores. Each develops into a pollen grain. Contrast with megasporogenesis where 3 of 4 megaspores degenerate."},{"id":1698,"text":"In a test cross between a heterozygous tall pea plant (Tt) and a homozygous dwarf plant (tt), 240 offspring are produced. As per the table below showing the expected phenotypic ratio, how many dwarf phenotype plants are expected?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"60"},{"label":"B","text":"120"},{"label":"C","text":"180"},{"label":"D","text":"240"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: A test cross (Tt × tt) always yields a 1:1 phenotypic ratio. Simply halve the total offspring (240/2 = 120). Step-by-step: The heterozygous parent produces gametes T and t (50% each). The recessive parent produces only t gametes. Offspring genotypes: Tt (tall) and tt (dwarf) in equal proportion. 50% of 240 = 120 dwarf plants."},{"id":1699,"text":"The C4 photosynthetic pathway requires additional ATP for the regeneration of phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) compared to the C3 pathway. Referring to the table below comparing the total ATP cost per CO2 fixed, how many extra ATP molecules are consumed per CO2 molecule in C4 plants?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"0"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Memorize the fixed difference: C4 pathway costs 2 extra ATP per CO2 fixed (total 5 ATP) vs C3 (total 3 ATP) due to PEP regeneration by pyruvate phosphate dikinase. Step-by-step: C3 cycle uses 3 ATP + 2 NADPH per CO2. C4 cycle adds 2 ATP for conversion of pyruvate to PEP in mesophyll cells. Total C4 = 5 ATP. Difference = 5 - 3 = 2 ATP."},{"id":1700,"text":"A diploid cell (2n = 14) in G2 phase (4C DNA content) enters Meiosis I. The table below tracks chromosome number and DNA content. What are the correct values for each daughter cell at the end of Telophase I?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7 chromosomes, 1C DNA"},{"label":"B","text":"7 chromosomes, 2C DNA"},{"label":"C","text":"14 chromosomes, 2C DNA"},{"label":"D","text":"14 chromosomes, 4C DNA"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Meiosis I is reductional division: Chromosome number halves (2n → n), DNA content halves (4C → 2C). Chromatids do not separate until Meiosis II. Step-by-step: G2 cell: 2n=14, 4C (replicated chromosomes). End of Telophase I: Homologous pairs separate. Each daughter cell gets 7 chromosomes (n=7), each still composed of 2 chromatids. DNA content is 2C."},{"id":1701,"text":"A restriction endonuclease recognizes a specific 6-base pair (bp) palindromic sequence. Assuming a random DNA sequence with equal base frequency (A, T, G, C), the table below shows the probability logic. What is the approximate average distance (in base pairs) between two consecutive restriction sites?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"256 bp"},{"label":"B","text":"1024 bp"},{"label":"C","text":"4096 bp"},{"label":"D","text":"16384 bp"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Frequency = 4^n where n = recognition site length. For 6 bp: 4^6 = 4096. Unit-digit trick: Powers of 4 end in 4 (odd power) or 6 (even power). 4^6 ends in 6. Only option ending in 6 is 4096. Step-by-step: Probability of specific base = 1/4. For 6 specific bases = (1/4)^6 = 1/4096. Average distance = 1 / Probability = 4096 bp."},{"id":1710,"text":"In C3 plants, under conditions of high temperature and low CO2 concentration, the oxygenase activity of RuBisCO catalyzes the reaction of RuBP with O2. What is the primary 2-carbon product formed in this reaction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3-Phosphoglycerate (PGA)"},{"label":"B","text":"Phosphoglycolate"},{"label":"C","text":"Ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate (RuBP)"},{"label":"D","text":"Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (G3P)"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: RuBisCO oxygenase splits the 5C RuBP into one 3C compound (PGA) and one 2C compound (Phosphoglycolate). The 2C product is the hallmark of photorespiration. Worked Solution: When RuBisCO acts as an oxygenase, RuBP (5C) + O2 → 3-Phosphoglycerate (3C) + 2-Phosphoglycolate (2C). Phosphoglycolate is subsequently metabolized in the photorespiratory cycle (C2 cycle) releasing CO2. PGA is the product of carboxylase activity (RuBP + CO2 → 2 PGA)."},{"id":1711,"text":"A crop plant shows distinct chlorosis and necrosis in the young, developing leaves while the older, mature leaves remain green and healthy. Based on the mobility of nutrients in the phloem, which nutrient deficiency is most likely the cause?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitrogen (N)"},{"label":"B","text":"Potassium (K)"},{"label":"C","text":"Calcium (Ca)"},{"label":"D","text":"Magnesium (Mg)"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: 'Young leaves affected = Immobile nutrient'; 'Old leaves affected = Mobile nutrient'. Calcium (Ca) and Boron (B) are classic phloem-immobile nutrients. N, K, Mg are highly mobile. Worked Solution: Mobile nutrients (N, P, K, Mg, Mo) are translocated from older senescing leaves to younger growing leaves via phloem; thus deficiency symptoms appear first on older leaves. Immobile nutrients (Ca, B, S, Fe, Zn, Cu) cannot bere mobilized; deficiency symptoms appear first on young leaves/meristems. Since symptoms are on young leaves, the deficient nutrient must be immobile. Among options, only Calcium is immobile."},{"id":1712,"text":"In a test cross between a dihybrid individual (AaBb) with linked genes and a double recessive parent (aabb), the progeny obtained were: 380 parental types (AB/ab) and 20 recombinant types (Ab/aB). What is the recombination frequency (map distance) between the two genes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2%"},{"label":"B","text":"5%"},{"label":"C","text":"10%"},{"label":"D","text":"20%"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: RF% = (Recombinants / Total Progeny) × 100. Total = 380 + 20 = 400. RF = (20/400) × 100 = 5%. Worked Solution: Recombination Frequency (RF) is calculated as the percentage of recombinant offspring in the total progeny. Total progeny = Parental (380) + Recombinant (20) = 400. Number of recombinants = 20. RF = (20 / 400) × 100 = 5%. This corresponds to a map distance of 5 centiMorgans (cM)."},{"id":1713,"text":"An angiosperm species has a diploid chromosome number (2n) of 24. During double fertilization, the second male gamete fuses with the two polar nuclei in the central cell to form the Primary Endosperm Nucleus (PEN). What is the chromosome number in the PEN?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12"},{"label":"B","text":"24"},{"label":"C","text":"36"},{"label":"D","text":"48"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: PEN is triploid (3n) formed by Triple Fusion: 2 Polar Nuclei (2n) + 1 Male Gamete (n) = 3n. Given 2n=24, n=12. PEN = 3 × 12 = 36. Worked Solution: Diploid number 2n = 24, so haploid number n = 12. The central cell contains two polar nuclei, each with n chromosomes (total 2n = 24). The male gamete carries n = 12 chromosomes. Fusion of these three nuclei (Triple Fusion) results in the Primary Endosperm Nucleus with 24 + 12 = 36 chromosomes (3n). Zygote is 2n (24), Antipodals/Synergids are n (12)."},{"id":1725,"text":"A plant is heterozygous at five independent loci (AaBbCcDdEe). As per the table below showing the relationship between the number of heterozygous loci (n) and the number of distinct gamete types (2ⁿ), how many genetically distinct types of gametes can this plant produce?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10"},{"label":"B","text":"25"},{"label":"C","text":"32"},{"label":"D","text":"64"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: For an organism heterozygous at 'n' independent loci, the number of distinct gamete types is 2ⁿ. Here n=5, so 2⁵ = 32. Step-by-step: Each locus (Aa, Bb, Cc, Dd, Ee) segregates independently into 2 allele types. By the Fundamental Principle of Counting, total combinations = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 32."},{"id":1726,"text":"The Respiratory Quotient (RQ) varies with the respiratory substrate. Referring to the table below showing typical RQ values for different substrates, what is the approximate RQ when tripalmitin (a fat) is the respiratory substrate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.7"},{"label":"B","text":"1.0"},{"label":"C","text":"1.3"},{"label":"D","text":"0.9"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Fats are reduced compounds requiring more O₂ for oxidation, yielding RQ ≈ 0.7. Carbohydrates = 1.0; Proteins ≈ 0.8–0.9; Organic acids > 1.0. Step-by-step: Tripalmitin (C₅₁H₉₈O₆) oxidation: C₅₁H₉₈O₆ + 72.5 O₂ → 51 CO₂ + 49 H₂O. RQ = 51/72.5 ≈ 0.703."},{"id":1727,"text":"During biological nitrogen fixation, the nitrogenase enzyme complex reduces one molecule of atmospheric nitrogen (N₂) to two molecules of ammonia (NH₃). How many molecules of ATP are hydrolysed per molecule of N₂ fixed?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8"},{"label":"B","text":"12"},{"label":"C","text":"16"},{"label":"D","text":"24"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Remember the fixed stoichiometry – nitrogenase requires 16 ATP per N₂ reduced. Full solution: The nitrogenase reaction consumes 16 MgATP per N₂ molecule to overcome the high bond energy of the N≡N triple bond, producing 2 NH₃, H₂, and 16 ADP + 16 Pi."},{"id":1728,"text":"Which plant hormone is primarily responsible for promoting stem elongation and breaking seed dormancy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Auxin"},{"label":"B","text":"Gibberellin"},{"label":"C","text":"Abscisic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"Ethylene"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: GA (Gibberellin) = growth and germination; ABA = dormancy. Full solution: Gibberellins stimulate stem elongation by promoting cell division and elongation, and they hydrolyse storage reserves in seeds to break dormancy, whereas abscisic acid enforces dormancy."},{"id":1740,"text":"In snapdragon (Antirrhinum majus), flower colour exhibits incomplete dominance. A cross between two F1 pink-flowered plants yields the phenotypic distribution shown in the table below. What is the expected phenotypic ratio in the F2 generation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3:1 (Dominant:Recessive)"},{"label":"B","text":"1:2:1 (Red:Pink:White)"},{"label":"C","text":"9:3:3:1 (Dihybrid)"},{"label":"D","text":"1:1 (Test cross ratio)"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Incomplete dominance yields a 1:2:1 phenotypic ratio in F2 (homozygous dominant : heterozygous : homozygous recessive). Worked: The F1 genotype is Rr (Pink). Selfing Rr × Rr gives genotypes RR (Red), Rr (Pink), rr (White) in 1:2:1 ratio. The observed data (30:62:28 ≈ 1:2:1) confirms this."},{"id":1741,"text":"The table below lists the solute potential (Ψs) and pressure potential (Ψp) for four adjacent plant cells. Using the formula Ψw = Ψs + Ψp, which cell has the highest (least negative) water potential?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cell A"},{"label":"B","text":"Cell B"},{"label":"C","text":"Cell C"},{"label":"D","text":"Cell D"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Water potential (Ψw) is highest when Ψs is least negative and Ψp is most positive; scan for the best combination. Worked: Calculate Ψw for each: A = -0.6, B = -0.5, C = -0.1, D = -0.4 MPa. Cell C (-0.1 MPa) is the least negative, hence highest water potential. Water moves from C to others."},{"id":1742,"text":"The bar chart below depicts the energy available at three trophic levels in a grassland ecosystem. Assuming Lindeman's 10% law of energy transfer applies, what is the approximate energy available at the Tertiary Consumer level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹"},{"label":"B","text":"1 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹"},{"label":"C","text":"100 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹"},{"label":"D","text":"0.1 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Apply the 10% rule: move decimal point one place left per trophic level. Worked: Producers (10,000) → Primary (1,000) → Secondary (100) → Tertiary (10). Energy at Tertiary level = 10% of 100 = 10 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹."},{"id":1743,"text":"The table below shows the exponential amplification of DNA during the first few cycles of a PCR reaction starting with one template molecule. Assuming 100% amplification efficiency per cycle, how many DNA molecules will be present after cycle 5?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"32"},{"label":"B","text":"64"},{"label":"C","text":"16"},{"label":"D","text":"25"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: PCR doubles DNA each cycle (2ⁿ). For n=5, 2⁵=32. Unit-digit trick: Powers of 2 end in 2,4,8,6 cycle; 2⁵ ends in 2. Only option A ends in 2. Worked: Cycle 0: 2⁰=1, Cycle 1: 2¹=2... Cycle 5: 2⁵=32 molecules."},{"id":1755,"text":"In a dihybrid cross (AaBb × AaBb), the F2 phenotypic ratio obtained is 9:3:4. This indicates recessive epistasis where the homozygous recessive genotype at the first locus (aa) masks the expression of the second locus. Which genotypic combination(s) correspond to the phenotypic class represented by '4' in this ratio?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A_B_"},{"label":"B","text":"aaB_ only"},{"label":"C","text":"A_bb only"},{"label":"D","text":"aaB_ and aabb (i.e., aa__)"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Recognize 9:3:4 as the classic recessive epistasis ratio. The '4' class always represents the homozygous recessive condition at the epistatic locus (aa), combining aaB_ (3) and aabb (1). Worked Solution: In recessive epistasis, genotype 'aa' masks the effect of 'B'. Standard F2 genotypic ratio is 9 A_B_ : 3 A_bb : 3 aaB_ : 1 aabb. Since aam asks B, both aaB_ (3 parts) and aabb (1 part) show the same phenotype, summing to 4 parts. This corresponds to the genotype aa__."},{"id":1756,"text":"A chloroplast suspension is illuminated with light of wavelength greater than 680 nm (activating only Photosystem I) in the absence of an external electron acceptor. Which set of products will be generated?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ATP, NADPH, and O₂"},{"label":"B","text":"ATP and NADPH only"},{"label":"C","text":"ATP only"},{"label":"D","text":"NADPH only"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Wavelength > 680 nm → Only PSI active → Cyclic Photophosphorylation → ATP only (No NADPH, No O₂). Worked Solution: Photosystem II (P680) absorbs light < 680 nm. Light > 680 nm excites only Photosystem I (P700). Without PSII activity, water is not split (no O₂, no electrons for NADP+ reduction). Electrons from PSI cycle back via ferredoxin and plastoquinone to P700 (Cyclic flow). This generates a proton gradient for ATP synthesis only. NADPH is not produced asn adp+ reductase requires electrons from then on-cyclic chain."},{"id":1757,"text":"As per the table below showing the number and arrangement of subsidiary cells in different stomatal types, which type is characterized by the absence of distinct subsidiary cells, being surrounded instead by epidermal cells indistinguishable from the rest?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Anomocytic"},{"label":"B","text":"Paracytic"},{"label":"C","text":"Diacytic"},{"label":"D","text":"Anisocytic"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: 'Anomo' = No distinct pattern/cells (irregular epidermal cells). 'Para' = 2 Parallel. 'Dia' = 2 Diagonal (perpendicular). 'Aniso' = 3 Unequal. Worked Solution: Anomocytic (irregular-celled) stomata lack specialized subsidiary cells; the guard cells are surrounded by a limited number of epidermal cells that are similar in size and shape to the other epidermal cells. Paracytic has two subsidiary cells parallel to the pore. Diacytic has two subsidiary cells perpendicular to the pore. Anisocytic has three subsidiary cells of unequal sizes."},{"id":1758,"text":"A linear DNA fragment of 5 kb is completely digested with the restriction enzyme EcoRI (recognition site: 5'-GAATTC-3'). The digestion yields three fragments of sizes 2 kb, 2 kb, and 1 kb. How many EcoRI recognition sites were present in the original linear fragment?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For LINEAR DNA: Number of Sites = Number of Fragments - 1. (For Circular DNA: Sites = Fragments). Here, 3 fragments → 3 - 1 = 2 sites. Worked Solution: A linear DNA molecule has two ends. Each cut at a restriction site increases the number of fragments by one. Starting with 1 fragment (uncut), 1 cut produces 2 fragments, and 2 cuts produce 3 fragments. Since the digestion produced 3 fragments (2 kb + 2 kb + 1 kb = 5 kb), exactly 2 cuts occurred, indicating 2 recognition sites for EcoRI in the original fragment."},{"id":1770,"text":"As per the table below comparing the ATP requirement for the synthesis of one molecule of glucose in C3 and C4 plants, how many additional ATP molecules are consumed in the C4 pathway due to the regeneration of phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) from pyruvate?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6"},{"label":"B","text":"12"},{"label":"C","text":"18"},{"label":"D","text":"24"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: The C4 cycle spends 2 extra ATP per CO₂ fixed to regenerate PEP from pyruvate via Pyruvate Phosphate Dikinase (PPDK). Since 6 CO₂ are fixed per glucose, extra ATP = 6 × 2 = 12. Step-by-step: In C3 plants, 1 glucose requires 18 ATP + 12 NADPH. In C4 plants, the CO₂ concentrating mechanism requires PEP regeneration. The reaction Pyruvate + ATP + Pi → PEP + AMP + PPi (hydrolyzed to 2Pi) costs 2 ATP equivalents per CO₂. For 6 CO₂ (1 glucose), this amounts to 12 additional ATP. Total C4 cost = 30 ATP. Difference = 30 - 18 = 12 ATP."},{"id":1771,"text":"As per the table below showing the dominant generation in the life cycles of major plant groups, which group exhibits a dominant, independent, free-living gametophytic generation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bryophytes"},{"label":"B","text":"Pteridophytes"},{"label":"C","text":"Gymnosperms"},{"label":"D","text":"Angiosperms"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Only Bryophytes (mosses, liverworts) have a dominant gametophyte. All vascular plants (Pteridophytes, Gymnosperms, Angiosperms) have a dominant sporophyte. Step-by-step: Examine the table. The row for Bryophytes lists 'Gametophyte' as the dominant generation. The rows for Pteridophytes, Gymnosperms, and Angiosperms all list 'Sporophyte'. The question asks for the group with a dominant gametophyte, which corresponds to the first row."},{"id":1772,"text":"The table below outlines the subcellular localization of key enzymes in the photorespiratory (C2) cycle. In which organelle is the enzyme glycine decarboxylase exclusively located, catalyzing the release of CO₂ and NH₃ from glycine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Chloroplast"},{"label":"B","text":"Peroxisome"},{"label":"C","text":"Mitochondrion"},{"label":"D","text":"Cytosol"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Glycine decarboxylase (GDC) is a mitochondrial matrix enzyme complex (similar to pyruvate dehydrogenase). It releases CO₂ and NH₃ specifically inside the mitochondrion during the conversion of glycine to serine. Step-by-step: Photorespiration starts in the chloroplast (RuBP oxygenation → Phosphoglycolate → Glycolate). Glycolate moves to the peroxisome (→ Glycine). Glycine moves to the mitochondrion where Glycine Decarboxylase (GDC) releases CO₂ and NH₃, forming Serine. Serine returns to the peroxisome. Thus, GDC is exclusively mitochondrial."},{"id":1773,"text":"A flowering plant possesses a syncarpous ovary where the septa are intact and the ovules are attached to the central axis formed by the fusion of carpel margins. As per the table below classifying placentation types, what is the placentation in this plant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Marginal"},{"label":"B","text":"Axile"},{"label":"C","text":"Parietal"},{"label":"D","text":"Free Central"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Syncarpous + Septa Present + Ovules on Central Axis = Axile. (Free Central = Septa Absent; Parietal = Ovules on Wall; Marginal = Monocarpous). Step-by-step: The description states 'syncarpous ovary' (fused carpels) with 'septa intact' (partitions present) and 'ovules attached to central axis'. This matches the definition of Axile placentation (e.g., Tomato, China Rose). In Parietal placentation, ovules are on the inner ovary wall. In Free Central, septa degenerate leaving a free central column. Marginal occurs in a single carpel (monocarpous)."}]},{"name":"Quantitative Aptitude","count":9,"questions":[{"id":1412,"text":"A particle moves in a circle of radius 2 m with speed 4 m/s. Its centripetal acceleration is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 m/s²"},{"label":"B","text":"8 m/s²"},{"label":"C","text":"4 m/s²"},{"label":"D","text":"16 m/s²"}],"answer":1,"solution":"a_c = v²/r = 16/2 = 8 m/s²."},{"id":1513,"text":"A particle moves in a circle of radius 2 m with speed 4 m/s. Its centripetal acceleration is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 m/s^2"},{"label":"B","text":"8 m/s^2"},{"label":"C","text":"4 m/s^2"},{"label":"D","text":"16 m/s^2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"a_c = v^2/r = 16/2 = 8 m/s^2."},{"id":1672,"text":"In a photoelectric effect experiment, the stopping potential (V_s) is plotted against the frequency (ν) of incident light. The slope of the straight line obtained gives:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Work function (φ)"},{"label":"B","text":"Planck's constant (h)"},{"label":"C","text":"Ratio h/e"},{"label":"D","text":"Threshold frequency (ν₀)"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Einstein's equation eV_s = hν - φ rearranges to V_s = (h/e)ν - φ/e; the slope is the coefficient of ν, which is h/e. Full Solution: The graph of stopping potential versus frequency is linear with slope h/e and intercept -φ/e on the V_s axis, directly yielding the ratio of Planck's constant to electronic charge."},{"id":1702,"text":"Two projectiles are launched with the same initial speed at angles of 30° and 60° with the horizontal. What is the ratio of their horizontal ranges (R₃₀ : R₆₀)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1:2"},{"label":"B","text":"1:1"},{"label":"C","text":"√3:1"},{"label":"D","text":"1:√3"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For complementary angles of projection (θ and 90°−θ) with same initial speed, the horizontal ranges are identical. Since 30° + 60° = 90°, the ratio is 1:1. Full Solution: Range formula R = u²sin(2θ)/g. For θ=30°, R₃₀ = u²sin(60°)/g = u²(√3/2)/g. For θ=60°, R₆₀ = u²sin(120°)/g = u²(√3/2)/g. Thus R₃₀/R₆₀ = 1."},{"id":1703,"text":"One mole each of a monoatomic gas (γ = 5/3) and a diatomic gas (γ = 7/5) undergo adiabatic expansion from the same initial state (P₁, V₁) to the same final volume V₂. The ratio of work done by the monoatomic gas to the diatomic gas (Wₘ : W_d) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5:3"},{"label":"B","text":"1:1"},{"label":"C","text":"3:5"},{"label":"D","text":"2:3"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Adiabatic work W = (P₁V₁ - P₂V₂)/(γ-1) = nR(T₁-T₂)/(γ-1). For same initial state and final volume, W ∝ 1/(γ-1). Mono: γ-1 = 2/3 ⇒ factor 3/2. Di: γ-1 = 2/5 ⇒ factor 5/2. Ratio = (3/2):(5/2) = 3:5. Full Solution: Using W = (nRΔT)/(γ-1) and adiabatic relation TV^(γ-1)=const, the temperature drop ΔT depends on γ. However, for fixed initial (P,V) and final V, the work ratio simplifies directly to the inverse ratio of (γ-1). Mono (γ=5/3) gives 1/(2/3)=1.5; Di (γ=7/5) gives 1/(2/5)=2.5. Ratio 1.5:2.5 = 3:5."},{"id":1720,"text":"The half‑life of a radioactive sample is 10 days. The fraction of the initial activity that remains after 30 days is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1/4"},{"label":"B","text":"1/16"},{"label":"C","text":"1/8"},{"label":"D","text":"1/2"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Number of half‑lives n = t/T½ = 30/10 = 3; fraction remaining = (1/2)ⁿ = 1/8. Full solution: Using N = N₀(1/2)^{t/T½}, after 30 days (3 half‑lives) N/N₀ = (1/2)³ = 1/8."},{"id":1735,"text":"Two projectiles are launched with the same initial speed u. The first is launched at an angle of 30° with the horizontal, and the second at an angle of 60°. The ratio of their maximum heights (H₁ : H₂) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 : 1"},{"label":"B","text":"1 : 3"},{"label":"C","text":"3 : 1"},{"label":"D","text":"1 : √3"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For complementary angles (θ and 90°−θ), maximum height ratio H₁/H₂ = tan²θ. Here θ=30°, tan30°=1/√3, so ratio = (1/√3)² = 1/3. Full Solution: Maximum height H = u²sin²θ/2g. For 30°, H₁ = u²(1/2)²/2g = u²/8g. For 60°, H₂ = u²(√3/2)²/2g = 3u²/8g. Ratio H₁:H₂ = 1:3."},{"id":1748,"text":"A rectangular coil of N turns and area A rotates at angular speed ω in a uniform magnetic field B. The maximum induced emf is ε_max. If the number of turns is doubled while the angular speed remains unchanged, the new maximum emf is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ε_max"},{"label":"B","text":"2ε_max"},{"label":"C","text":"4ε_max"},{"label":"D","text":"ε_max/2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Maximum emf ε_max = NBAω, directly proportional to N; doubling N doubles ε_max. Full solution: Original ε_max = NBAω. New N' = 2N, ω' = ω → ε'_max = N'BAω' = (2N)BAω = 2(NBAω) = 2ε_max."},{"id":1750,"text":"One mole of a monoatomic ideal gas (γ = 5/3) undergoes an adiabatic expansion until its volume becomes 8 times the initial volume. The final temperature T_f as a fraction of the initial temperature T_i is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1/2"},{"label":"B","text":"1/8"},{"label":"C","text":"1/16"},{"label":"D","text":"1/4"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: For adiabatic process TV^(γ-1) = constant. With γ = 5/3, γ-1 = 2/3. Volume ratio V_f/V_i = 8 ⇒ T_f/T_i = (V_i/V_f)^(γ-1) = (1/8)^(2/3) = (2⁻³)^(2/3) = 2⁻² = 1/4. Full solution: T_i V_i^(2/3) = T_f V_f^(2/3) → T_f = T_i (V_i/V_f)^(2/3) = T_i (1/8)^(2/3) = T_i/4."}]}]};